<h3> ELEMENT FAMILIES</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Alkali Metals:</strong> Li, Na, K</li>
  <li><strong>Alkaline Earth Metals:</strong> Be, Mg, Ca</li>
  <li><strong>Halogens:</strong> F, Cl</li>
  <li><strong>Noble Gases (Inert Gases):</strong> He, Ne, Ar</li>
  <li><strong>Metalloids:</strong> B, Si</li>
  <li><strong>Nonmetals:</strong> H, C, N, O, P, S</li>
</ul>

  <h3> BLOCKS OF PERIODIC TABLE</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>s-block:</strong> Groups 1–2 (highly reactive metals)</li>
    <li><strong>p-block:</strong> Groups 13–18 (non-metals + noble gases)</li>
    <li><strong>d-block:</strong> Transition metals (hard, good conductors, variable charges)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> GROUPS / FAMILIES</h3>

  <h4> Group 1: Alkali Metals</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Very reactive metals</li>
    <li>Reactivity increases down the group</li>
  </ul>

  <h4> Group 2: Alkaline Earth Metals</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Reactive metals but less than Group 1</li>
    <li>Reactivity increases down the group</li>
  </ul>

  <h4> Group 17: Halogens</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Very reactive non-metals</li>
    <li>Reactivity decreases down the group</li>
    <li>Form -1 ions (e.g. Cl⁻)</li>
  </ul>

  <h4> Group 18: Noble Gases</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Inert (very unreactive)</li>
    <li>Full outer shell</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> PERIODIC TRENDS</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Atomic radius:</strong> decreases across a period → increases down a group</li>
    <li><strong>Reactivity (metals):</strong> increases down a group</li>
    <li><strong>Reactivity (non-metals):</strong> decreases down a group</li>
    <li><strong>Ionization energy:</strong> increases across a period</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> ELECTRON NOTATION (Z, K, L, M)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Na (11): 2,8,1</li>
    <li>Cl (17): 2,8,7</li>
    <li>Mg (12): 2,8,2</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (125, 125, '<h2>Chemical Bonding</h2>
<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Chemical bonding is the force of attraction that holds atoms together in a molecule or compound.</li>
<li>Atoms bond to achieve a stable electronic configuration (usually a full outer shell).</li>
<li>Stability is achieved by gaining, losing, or sharing electrons.</li>
<li>Main types of bonding: Ionic bonding, Covalent bonding, Metallic bonding.</li>
</ul>

<h3> TYPES OF BONDING (SIMPLIFIED)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Ionic bonding:</b> Transfer of electrons (metal + non-metal)</li>
<li><b>Covalent bonding:</b> Sharing of electrons (non-metal + non-metal)</li>
<li><b>Metallic bonding:</b> Sea of free electrons in metals</li>
</ul>

<h3> SIMPLE MODEL</h3>
<pre>
Atoms → Electron change → Stable compound
</pre>
', 1),
  (126, 126, '<h2>Ionic Bonding</h2>
<h3> IONIC BOND FORMATION MODEL</h3>

<p>
An <strong>ionic bond</strong> forms when electrons are transferred from a metal atom to a non-metal atom. 
This creates oppositely charged ions that attract each other strongly.
</p>

<table border="1" cellpadding="8" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse: collapse; text-align:center; width:100%;">

<tr>
  <th>Step</th>
  <th>Process</th>
  <th>Result</th>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td>1</td>
  <td>Metal atom loses electrons</td>
  <td>Forms a positive ion (<strong>cation</strong>)</td>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td>2</td>
  <td>Non-metal atom gains electrons</td>
  <td>Forms a negative ion (<strong>anion</strong>)</td>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td>3</td>
  <td>Oppositely charged ions attract</td>
  <td>Forms an <strong>ionic compound</strong></td>
</tr>

</table>

<h3> GENERAL FORMATION MODEL</h3>

<pre>
Metal atom  →  loses electron(s)  →  Cation (+)

Non-metal atom  →  gains electron(s)  →  Anion (−)

Cation (+)  +  Anion (−)  →  Ionic Compound
</pre>

<h3> EXAMPLE: FORMATION OF SODIUM CHLORIDE (NaCl)</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="8" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse: collapse; text-align:center; width:100%;">

<tr>
  <th>Reaction Step</th>
  <th>Explanation</th>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td><strong>Na → Na⁺ + e⁻</strong></td>
  <td>Sodium loses one electron to form a positive sodium ion.</td>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td><strong>Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻</strong></td>
  <td>Chlorine gains one electron to form a negative chloride ion.</td>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td><strong>Na⁺ + Cl⁻ → NaCl</strong></td>
  <td>The oppositely charged ions attract to form sodium chloride.</td>
</tr>

</table>

<h3> KEY NOTES</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Cations</strong> are positively charged ions formed by loss of electrons.</li>
  <li><strong>Anions</strong> are negatively charged ions formed by gain of electrons.</li>
  <li>Ionic bonding usually occurs between a <strong>metal</strong> and a <strong>non-metal</strong>.</li>
  <li>The electrostatic attraction between ions forms a strong ionic bond.</li>
  <li>Ionic compounds usually have high melting and boiling points.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (127, 127, '<h2>Covalent Bonding</h2>
<h3> COVALENT BONDING</h3>

<p>
Covalent bonding is a type of chemical bonding in which atoms share electrons in order to achieve stable outer energy levels. 
This type of bonding occurs mainly between <strong>non-metal atoms</strong>.
</p>

<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Covalent bonding</strong> is the sharing of electrons between atoms.</li>

  <li>It occurs mainly between <strong>non-metal + non-metal</strong> atoms.</li>

  <li>Each atom contributes one or more electrons to form a <strong>shared pair of electrons</strong>.</li>

  <li>The shared electrons are attracted by the nuclei of both atoms, which holds the atoms together strongly.</li>

  <li>Covalent bonding helps atoms achieve a <strong>stable outer energy level</strong> similar to noble gases.</li>

  <li>Covalent bonds form substances called <strong>molecules</strong>, such as H₂O, O₂, and CO₂.</li>

  <li>Some covalent substances form <strong>giant covalent structures</strong>, such as diamond and graphite.</li>
</ul>

<h3> KEY IDEAS</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="8" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse: collapse; width:100%; text-align:center;">

<tr>
  <th>Concept</th>
  <th>Explanation</th>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td><strong>Non-metal + Non-metal</strong></td>
  <td>Forms a covalent bond</td>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td><strong>Electron Sharing</strong></td>
  <td>Electrons are shared, not transferred</td>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td><strong>Bond Strength</strong></td>
  <td>Depends on the number of shared electron pairs</td>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td><strong>Molecule Formation</strong></td>
  <td>Atoms join together to form molecules</td>
</tr>

</table>

<h3> TYPES OF COVALENT BONDS</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="8" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse: collapse; width:100%; text-align:center;">

<tr>
  <th>Bond Type</th>
  <th>Electron Pairs Shared</th>
  <th>Symbol</th>
  <th>Strength</th>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td><strong>Single Bond</strong></td>
  <td>1 pair</td>
  <td>—</td>
  <td>Weakest of the three</td>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td><strong>Double Bond</strong></td>
  <td>2 pairs</td>
  <td>=</td>
  <td>Stronger than a single bond</td>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td><strong>Triple Bond</strong></td>
  <td>3 pairs</td>
  <td>≡</td>
  <td>Strongest covalent bond</td>
</tr>

</table>

<h3> COVALENT BOND MODELS</h3>

<p>
Atoms combine by sharing electrons to form covalent bonds. 
The number of shared electron pairs determines whether the bond is single, double, or triple.
</p>

<table border="1" cellpadding="8" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse: collapse; text-align:center; width:100%;">

<tr>
  <th>Atoms Joining</th>
  <th>Molecule Formed</th>
  <th>Bond Type</th>
  <th>Electrons Shared</th>
  <th>Example Structure</th>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td>Hydrogen + Hydrogen</td>
  <td><strong>H₂</strong></td>
  <td>Single Bond</td>
  <td>1 pair (2 electrons)</td>
  <td>H — H</td>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td>Oxygen + Oxygen</td>
  <td><strong>O₂</strong></td>
  <td>Double Bond</td>
  <td>2 pairs (4 electrons)</td>
  <td>O = O</td>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td>Nitrogen + Nitrogen</td>
  <td><strong>N₂</strong></td>
  <td>Triple Bond</td>
  <td>3 pairs (6 electrons)</td>
  <td>N ≡ N</td>
</tr>

</table>

<h3> IMPORTANT NOTES</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Single bonds</strong> involve one shared pair of electrons.</li>

  <li><strong>Double bonds</strong> involve two shared pairs of electrons.</li>

  <li><strong>Triple bonds</strong> involve three shared pairs of electrons.</li>

  <li>The more electrons shared, the <strong>stronger</strong> the bond becomes.</li>

  <li>Triple bonds are generally shorter and stronger than double and single bonds.</li>

  <li>Covalent compounds usually have <strong>low melting and boiling points</strong>, except giant covalent structures.</li>

  <li>Most covalent substances do <strong>not conduct electricity</strong> because they lack free-moving charged particles.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (128, 128, '<h2>Ionic vs Covalent Bonding</h2>
<h3> COMPARISON BETWEEN IONIC AND COVALENT BONDING</h3>

<p>
Ionic and covalent bonding are the two main types of chemical bonding. 
Both help atoms achieve stable outer energy levels, but they differ in the way electrons behave.
</p>

<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>

<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Ionic bonding</strong> involves the <strong>transfer of electrons</strong> from a metal atom to a non-metal atom, forming charged particles called ions.
  </li>

  <li>
    <strong>Covalent bonding</strong> involves the <strong>sharing of electrons</strong> between non-metal atoms.
  </li>

  <li>
    In ionic bonding, atoms become <strong>cations (+)</strong> and <strong>anions (−)</strong>, which attract each other through electrostatic forces.
  </li>

  <li>
    In covalent bonding, atoms remain neutral because the electrons are shared rather than transferred.
  </li>

  <li>
    Ionic compounds usually form large, regular <strong>crystal lattice structures</strong>.
  </li>

  <li>
    Covalent compounds usually form <strong>molecules</strong>, although some form giant covalent structures like diamond.
  </li>

  <li>
    Ionic bonds are generally very strong because of the strong attraction between opposite charges.
  </li>

  <li>
    Covalent bond strength depends on the number of shared electron pairs.
  </li>
</ul>

<h3> CORE DIFFERENCE</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="8" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse: collapse; width:100%; text-align:center;">

<tr>
  <th>Ionic Bonding</th>
  <th>Covalent Bonding</th>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td>Electrons are transferred</td>
  <td>Electrons are shared</td>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td>Occurs between metal + non-metal</td>
  <td>Occurs between non-metal + non-metal</td>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td>Forms ions</td>
  <td>Forms molecules</td>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td>Strong electrostatic attraction</td>
  <td>Shared electron attraction</td>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td>Usually forms crystal lattices</td>
  <td>Usually forms simple molecules</td>
</tr>

</table>

<h3> SIMPLE FORMATION MODELS</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="8" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse: collapse; width:100%; text-align:center;">

<tr>
  <th>Bond Type</th>
  <th>Formation Process</th>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td><strong>Ionic Bonding</strong></td>
  <td>Metal → Non-metal (electron transfer)</td>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td><strong>Covalent Bonding</strong></td>
  <td>Non-metal + Non-metal (electron sharing)</td>
</tr>

</table>

<h3> EXAMPLES OF EACH TYPE</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="8" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse: collapse; width:100%; text-align:center;">

<tr>
  <th>Bond Type</th>
  <th>Examples</th>
  <th>Description</th>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td><strong>Ionic Compounds</strong></td>
  <td>NaCl, MgO, CaCl₂</td>
  <td>Made from metal and non-metal ions</td>
</tr>

<tr>
  <td><strong>Covalent Compounds</strong></td>
  <td>H₂, O₂, CH₄</td>
  <td>Made from shared electrons between non-metals</td>
</tr>

</table>

<h3> IMPORTANT DIFFERENCES</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Ionic compounds</strong> usually have high melting and boiling points.</li>

  <li><strong>Covalent compounds</strong> usually have lower melting and boiling points.</li>

  <li>Ionic compounds conduct electricity when molten or dissolved in water.</li>

  <li>Most covalent compounds do not conduct electricity.</li>

  <li>Ionic compounds are often hard and brittle.</li>

  <li>Covalent substances may exist as gases, liquids, or soft solids.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (129, 129, '<h2>Meaning of Mole</h2>
<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<p>
A mole is the SI unit used in chemistry to measure the amount of substance.
It is similar to how we use “dozen” for 12 items, but a mole is much larger.
</p>
<ul>
<li>1 mole contains <b>6.02 × 10²³ particles</b></li>
<li>This number is called <b>Avogadro’s constant</b></li>
<li>Particles may be atoms, molecules, or ions</li>
</ul>
<h3> DEEP UNDERSTANDING</h3>
<p>
Atoms are extremely small, so counting them individually is impossible.
The mole acts as a “bridge” between the microscopic world (atoms) and the macroscopic world (grams in the lab).
</p>
<h3> IMPORTANT RELATIONSHIPS</h3>
<pre>
1 mole = 6.02 × 10²³ particles
n = m / M
(where n = moles, m = mass, M = molar mass)
Number of particles = n × 6.02 × 10²³
</pre>
<h3> TYPES OF PARTICLES IN A MOLE</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Atoms:</b> He, Fe, Na</li>
<li><b>Molecules:</b> H₂O, CO₂, O₂</li>
<li><b>Ions:</b> Na⁺, Cl⁻</li>
</ul>
<h3> WHY THE MOLE IS IMPORTANT</h3>
<ul>
<li>Used to balance chemical equations quantitatively</li>
<li>Helps calculate reactants and products in reactions</li>
<li>Connects mass measurements to atomic scale</li>
<li>Essential in industrial chemistry and medicine</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (130, 130, '<h2>Mole Relationships</h2>
<h3> MOLE RELATIONSHIPS</h3>
<p>
Mole relationships show how we convert between <strong>mass (g)</strong>, <strong>moles (mol)</strong>, and <strong>particles</strong> (atoms, molecules, ions).
</p>
<p>
These conversions are important because atoms are too small to measure directly.
</p>
<hr>
<h3> KEY CONVERSIONS</h3>
<pre>
n = m ÷ M
m = n × M
N = n × 6.02 × 10²³
n = N ÷ 6.02 × 10²³
</pre>
<p><strong>Where:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>n = moles</li>
  <li>m = mass (g)</li>
  <li>M = molar mass (g/mol)</li>
  <li>N = number of particles</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> SIMPLE IDEA</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Mass ↔ Moles (use molar mass)</li>
  <li>Moles ↔ Particles (use Avogadro’s number)</li>
</ul>
<pre>
1 mole = 6.02 × 10²³ particles
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> EXAMPLE 1: MASS → MOLES</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Find moles in 18 g of water (H₂O)</p>
<h4>Step 1: Write formula</h4>
<pre>n = m ÷ M</pre>
<h4>Step 2: Identify values</h4>
<pre>
m = 18 g
M = 18 g/mol
</pre>
<h4>Step 3: Substitute</h4>
<pre>
n = 18 ÷ 18
</pre>
<h4>Step 4: Calculate</h4>
<pre>
n = 1 mol
</pre>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 1 mole</p>
<hr>
<h3> EXAMPLE 2: MOLES → PARTICLES</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Find particles in 1 mole of CO₂</p>
<h4>Step 1: Formula</h4>
<pre>N = n × 6.02 × 10²³</pre>
<h4>Step 2: Substitute</h4>
<pre>
N = 1 × 6.02 × 10²³
</pre>
<h4>Step 3: Calculate</h4>
<pre>
N = 6.02 × 10²³ particles
</pre>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 6.02 × 10²³ particles</p>
<hr>
<h3> EXAMPLE 3: PARTICLES → MOLES</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Convert 1.204 × 10²⁴ particles to moles</p>
<h4>Step 1: Formula</h4>
<pre>n = N ÷ 6.02 × 10²³</pre>
<h4>Step 2: Substitute</h4>
<pre>
n = (1.204 × 10²⁴) ÷ (6.02 × 10²³)
</pre>
<h4>Step 3: Calculate</h4>
<pre>
n = 2 mol
</pre>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2 moles</p>
<hr>
<h3> QUICK UNDERSTANDING</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Mass uses molar mass</li>
  <li>Moles use ratios</li>
  <li>Particles use Avogadro’s number</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> QUICK SUMMARY</h3>
<pre>
Mass ↔ Moles: n = m ÷ M
Moles ↔ Particles: N = n × 6.02 × 10²³
</pre>
', 1),
  (131, 131, '<h2>Molar Mass</h2>
<h3> MOLAR MASS</h3>
<p>
Molar mass is the mass of 1 mole of a substance measured in <strong>g/mol</strong>.
</p>
<hr>
<h3> CORE IDEA</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Add atomic masses of all atoms in a formula</li>
  <li>Unit is always g/mol</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Molar Mass = Sum of all atomic masses in the formula
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> BASIC METHOD</h3>
<pre>
1. Write formula
2. Count atoms
3. Multiply by atomic masses
4. Add everything
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> EXAMPLE 1: WATER (H₂O)</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Find molar mass of H₂O</p>
<h4>Step 1: Write formula</h4>
<pre>H₂O</pre>
<h4>Step 2: Count atoms</h4>
<pre>
H = 2
O = 1
</pre>
<h4>Step 3: Multiply atomic masses</h4>
<pre>
H = 1 × 2 = 2
O = 16 × 1 = 16
</pre>
<h4>Step 4: Add</h4>
<pre>
2 + 16 = 18 g/mol
</pre>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 18 g/mol</p>
<hr>
<h3> EXAMPLE 2: CARBON DIOXIDE (CO₂)</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Find molar mass of CO₂</p>
<h4>Step 1: Formula</h4>
<pre>CO₂</pre>
<h4>Step 2: Count atoms</h4>
<pre>
C = 1
O = 2
</pre>
<h4>Step 3: Multiply</h4>
<pre>
C = 12 × 1 = 12
O = 16 × 2 = 32
</pre>
<h4>Step 4: Add</h4>
<pre>
12 + 32 = 44 g/mol
</pre>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 44 g/mol</p>
<hr>
<h3> EXAMPLE 3: SODIUM CHLORIDE (NaCl)</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Find molar mass of NaCl</p>
<h4>Step 1: Formula</h4>
<pre>NaCl</pre>
<h4>Step 2: Count atoms</h4>
<pre>
Na = 1
Cl = 1
</pre>
<h4>Step 3: Multiply</h4>
<pre>
Na = 23 × 1 = 23
Cl = 35.5 × 1 = 35.5
</pre>
<h4>Step 4: Add</h4>
<pre>
23 + 35.5 = 58.5 g/mol
</pre>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 58.5 g/mol</p>
<hr>
<h3> HOW IT CONNECTS TO MOLES</h3>
<p>Once you know molar mass, you can calculate moles:</p>
<pre>
n = m ÷ M
</pre>
<p>
Where:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>n = moles</li>
  <li>m = mass (g)</li>
  <li>M = molar mass (g/mol)</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> QUICK SUMMARY</h3>
<pre>
1. Count atoms
2. Multiply by atomic mass
3. Add totals
4. Use in n = m ÷ M
</pre>
', 1),
  (132, 132, '<h2> STOICHIOMETRY </h2>
<h3> STOICHIOMETRY</h3>
<h3> DEFINITION</h3>
<p>
Stoichiometry is the calculation of amounts of reactants and products in chemical reactions using balanced equations and mole ratios.
</p>
<hr>
<h3> MAIN IDEA</h3>
<ul>
  <li>The balanced equation gives the <strong>mole ratio</strong></li>
  <li>Moles are used to connect mass, volume, and particles</li>
  <li>All calculations depend on the mole concept</li>
</ul>
<pre>
2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O
Ratio = 2 : 1 : 2
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> BASIC METHOD</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Step 1:</strong> Balance the equation</li>
  <li><strong>Step 2:</strong> Convert values to moles</li>
  <li><strong>Step 3:</strong> Use mole ratio</li>
  <li><strong>Step 4:</strong> Convert to required unit (mass, volume, particles)</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> MOLE FORMULAS</h3>
<pre>
n = mass ÷ molar mass
mass = moles × molar mass
particles = moles × 6.02 × 10²³
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> EXAMPLE 1: MOLES FROM MASS</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Find moles in 10 g of oxygen gas (O₂)</p>
<h4>Step 1: Write formula</h4>
<pre>
n = mass ÷ molar mass
</pre>
<h4>Step 2: Substitute values</h4>
<pre>
M(O₂) = 32
n = 10 ÷ 32
</pre>
<h4>Step 3: Calculate</h4>
<pre>
n = 0.3125 mol
</pre>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 0.3125 mol</p>
<hr>
<h3> EXAMPLE 2: MASS–MASS STOICHIOMETRY</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. Find mass of water formed from 4 g of H₂.</p>
<h4>Step 1: Convert H₂ to moles</h4>
<pre>
n = 4 ÷ 2 = 2 mol
</pre>
<h4>Step 2: Use mole ratio</h4>
<pre>
2H₂ : 2H₂O → 1 : 1 ratio
So 2 mol H₂ → 2 mol H₂O
</pre>
<h4>Step 3: Convert moles of water to mass</h4>
<pre>
Mass = 2 × 18 = 36 g
</pre>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 36 g of water</p>
<hr>
<h3> KEY POINTS</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Always start with a balanced equation</li>
  <li>Convert everything to moles first</li>
  <li>Use ratios from coefficients</li>
  <li>Convert back to required unit at the end</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Not balancing the equation first</li>
  <li>Using mass instead of moles in ratios</li>
  <li>Skipping unit conversions</li>
  <li>Ignoring coefficients in equations</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> QUICK SUMMARY</h3>
<pre>
1. Balance equation
2. Convert to moles
3. Use ratio
4. Convert to final answer
</pre>
', 1),
  (133, 133, '<h2> EMPIRICAL FORMULA MASTER PACK</h2>
<hr>
<h3> EMPIRICAL FORMULA</h3>
<h3> DEFINITION</h3>
<p>
The <strong>empirical formula</strong> is the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms of each element in a compound.
</p>
<hr>
<h3> MAIN IDEA</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Convert given values to moles</li>
  <li>Divide all by the smallest number of moles</li>
  <li>Adjust to whole numbers if needed</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Empirical Formula = Simplest Ratio of atoms
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> METHOD (STEP BY STEP)</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Step 1:</strong> If percentage is given, assume 100 g</li>
  <li><strong>Step 2:</strong> Convert mass to moles</li>
  <li><strong>Step 3:</strong> Divide all mole values by the smallest one</li>
  <li><strong>Step 4:</strong> Multiply if needed to get whole numbers</li>
  <li><strong>Step 5:</strong> Write final formula</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> EXAMPLE 1: C, H, O COMPOUND</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Find empirical formula for 40% C, 6.7% H, 53.3% O</p>
<h4>Step 1: Assume 100 g</h4>
<pre>
C = 40 g
H = 6.7 g
O = 53.3 g
</pre>
<h4>Step 2: Convert to moles</h4>
<pre>
C = 40 ÷ 12 = 3.33
H = 6.7 ÷ 1 = 6.7
O = 53.3 ÷ 16 = 3.33
</pre>
<h4>Step 3: Divide by smallest</h4>
<pre>
3.33 : 6.7 : 3.33
÷ 3.33
= 1 : 2 : 1
</pre>
<h4>Step 4: Write formula</h4>
<pre>
CH₂O
</pre>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> CH₂O</p>
<hr>
<h3> EXAMPLE 2: IRON OXIDE</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> 70% Fe, 30% O</p>
<h4>Step 1: Assume 100 g</h4>
<pre>
Fe = 70 g
O = 30 g
</pre>
<h4>Step 2: Convert to moles</h4>
<pre>
Fe = 70 ÷ 56 = 1.25
O = 30 ÷ 16 = 1.875
</pre>
<h4>Step 3: Divide by smallest</h4>
<pre>
1.25 : 1.875
÷ 1.25
= 1 : 1.5
</pre>
<h4>Step 4: Make whole numbers</h4>
<pre>
1 : 1.5 × 2 = 2 : 3
</pre>
<h4>Step 5: Write formula</h4>
<pre>
Fe₂O₃
</pre>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Fe₂O₃</p>
<hr>
<h3> EXAMPLE 3: WATER</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> 2 g H and 16 g O</p>
<h4>Step 1: Convert to moles</h4>
<pre>
H = 2 ÷ 1 = 2
O = 16 ÷ 16 = 1
</pre>
<h4>Step 2: Ratio</h4>
<pre>
2 : 1
</pre>
<h4>Step 3: Write formula</h4>
<pre>
H₂O
</pre>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> H₂O</p>
<hr>
<h3> COMMON FIXES (VERY IMPORTANT)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>1.5 → multiply by 2</li>
  <li>2.5 → multiply by 2</li>
  <li>1.33 → multiply by 3</li>
  <li>1.25 → multiply by 4</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Not converting to moles first</li>
  <li>Dividing by the wrong number</li>
  <li>Leaving decimal ratios</li>
  <li>Forgetting to multiply to get whole numbers</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> QUICK SUMMARY</h3>
<pre>
1. Assume 100 g (if % given)
2. Convert to moles
3. Divide by smallest
4. Make whole numbers
5. Write formula
</pre>
', 1),
  (134, 134, '<h2> MOLECULAR FORMULA MASTER PACK</h2>
<hr>
<h3> MOLECULAR FORMULA</h3>
<h3> DEFINITION</h3>
<p>
A <strong>molecular formula</strong> shows the actual number of atoms of each element in a compound.
It tells the <strong>true composition</strong> of the substance.
</p>
<p>
It is always a multiple of the <strong>empirical formula</strong>.
</p>
<hr>
<h3> MAIN IDEA</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Start from the empirical formula</li>
  <li>Use molar mass (Mr)</li>
  <li>Find how many times bigger the real formula is</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Molecular Formula = Empirical Formula × n
n = Mr ÷ Empirical Formula Mass
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> METHOD (VERY IMPORTANT STEPS)</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write empirical formula</li>
  <li><strong>Step 2:</strong> Find empirical formula mass (EFM)</li>
  <li><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate n = Mr ÷ EFM</li>
  <li><strong>Step 4:</strong> Multiply whole formula by n</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> EXAMPLE 1: CH₂O</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Empirical formula = CH₂O, Mr = 60. Find molecular formula.</p>
<h4>Step 1: Empirical formula</h4>
<pre>CH₂O</pre>
<h4>Step 2: EFM</h4>
<pre>
C = 12
H = 1 × 2 = 2
O = 16
EFM = 12 + 2 + 16 = 30
</pre>
<h4>Step 3: Find n</h4>
<pre>
n = 60 ÷ 30 = 2
</pre>
<h4>Step 4: Multiply everything by 2</h4>
<pre>
CH₂O × 2 = C₂H₄O₂
</pre>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C₂H₄O₂</p>
<hr>
<h3> EXAMPLE 2: CH</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Empirical formula = CH, Mr = 78</p>
<h4>Step 1: Empirical formula</h4>
<pre>CH</pre>
<h4>Step 2: EFM</h4>
<pre>
C = 12
H = 1
EFM = 13
</pre>
<h4>Step 3: Find n</h4>
<pre>
n = 78 ÷ 13 = 6
</pre>
<h4>Step 4: Multiply formula</h4>
<pre>
CH × 6 = C₆H₆
</pre>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C₆H₆</p>
<hr>
<h3> KEY POINTS</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Molecular formula = real number of atoms</li>
  <li>Empirical formula = simplest ratio</li>
  <li>n must always be a whole number</li>
  <li>Multiply EVERY atom, not just one element</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Skipping empirical formula step</li>
  <li>Wrong EFM calculation</li>
  <li>Dividing incorrectly for n</li>
  <li>Multiplying only one element instead of whole formula</li>
  <li>Leaving answer in empirical form</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> QUICK SUMMARY</h3>
<pre>
1. Write empirical formula
2. Find EFM
3. n = Mr ÷ EFM
4. Multiply entire formula by n
</pre>
', 1),
  (135, 135, '<h2> LIMITING REAGENT MASTER PACK</h2>
<h3> LIMITING REAGENT</h3>
<h3> DEFINITION</h3>
<p>
The <strong>limiting reagent</strong> is the reactant that is used up first in a reaction and stops the reaction from continuing.
It controls how much product is formed.
</p>
<hr>
<h3> HOW TO FIND IT (STEPS)</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write and balance the equation</li>
  <li><strong>Step 2:</strong> Convert all reactants to moles</li>
  <li><strong>Step 3:</strong> Compare mole ratios using coefficients</li>
  <li><strong>Step 4:</strong> The reactant that gives the smallest result is the limiting reagent</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> EXAMPLE 1: HYDROGEN + OXYGEN</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Find the limiting reagent when 4 mol H₂ reacts with 1 mol O₂.</p>
<h4>Step 1: Balanced equation</h4>
<pre>
2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O
</pre>
<h4>Step 2: Compare ratio</h4>
<p>
From the equation:
</p>
<pre>
2 mol H₂ needs 1 mol O₂
</pre>
<p>
Now compare with what we have:
</p>
<pre>
H₂ = 4 mol
O₂ = 1 mol
</pre>
<p>
Check how much H₂ is needed for 1 mol O₂:
</p>
<pre>
1 mol O₂ needs 2 mol H₂ (we have 4 mol H₂ → excess)
</pre>
<p>
So oxygen runs out first.
</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> O₂ is the limiting reagent</p>
<hr>
<h3> EXAMPLE 2: CALCIUM CARBONATE</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂ (1 mol CaCO₃ used)</p>
<p>
There is only <strong>one reactant</strong>, so nothing to compare with.
</p>
<p>
It is completely used to form products.
</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> No limiting reagent</p>
<hr>
<h3> EXAMPLE 3: MAGNESIUM + OXYGEN</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> 2 mol Mg reacts with 1 mol O₂</p>
<h4>Step 1: Balanced equation</h4>
<pre>
2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO
</pre>
<h4>Step 2: Compare</h4>
<pre>
Mg = 2 mol
O₂ = 1 mol
</pre>
<p>
This matches the exact ratio in the equation.
</p>
<p>
So both react completely.
</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> No limiting reagent (complete reaction)</p>
<hr>
<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Forgetting to balance the equation first</li>
  <li>Using mass instead of moles</li>
  <li>Ignoring coefficients in the equation</li>
  <li>Thinking the smallest number of moles is always limiting (not true)</li>
  <li>Not comparing ratios properly</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (136, 136, '<h2> GAS VOLUME (CORE)</h2>
<p>
<h3> MOLAR GAS VOLUME AT STP</h3>
<h3> DEFINITION</h3>
<p>
At <strong>Standard Temperature and Pressure (STP)</strong>, 
1 mole of any gas occupies a volume of <strong>22.4 dm³</strong>.
</p>
<p>
This relationship allows conversion between the <strong>number of moles</strong> and the <strong>volume of gases</strong>.
</p>
<hr>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<ul>
  <li>1 mole of gas = 22.4 dm³ at STP</li>
  <li>Applies only under standard conditions (STP)</li>
  <li>Used in gas calculations in chemistry</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> FORMULAS</h3>
<p><strong>Volume of gas:</strong></p>
<pre>
Volume = moles × 22.4
</pre>
<p><strong>Number of moles:</strong></p>
<pre>
Moles = Volume ÷ 22.4
</pre>
<p><strong>Where:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>Volume is in dm³</li>
  <li>Moles is in mol</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Find the volume of 2 moles of oxygen gas at STP.</p>
<p><strong>Solution:</strong></p>
<pre>
Volume = moles × 22.4
Volume = 2 × 22.4
Volume = 44.8 dm³
</pre>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 44.8 dm³</p>
<hr>
<h3> WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Find the volume of 0.5 moles of hydrogen gas at STP.</p>
<p><strong>Solution:</strong></p>
<pre>
Volume = 0.5 × 22.4
Volume = 11.2 dm³
</pre>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 11.2 dm³</p>
<hr>
<h3> WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> A gas occupies 67.2 dm³ at STP. Find the number of moles.</p>
<p><strong>Solution:</strong></p>
<pre>
Moles = Volume ÷ 22.4
Moles = 67.2 ÷ 22.4
Moles = 3 mol
</pre>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 3 mol</p>
<hr>
<h3> MASTER RULE</h3>
<p>
At STP, all gas calculations depend on:
</p>
<pre>
1 mole = 22.4 dm³
</pre>
<p>
Always convert between moles and volume using this relationship.
</p>
<hr>
<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Using 22.4 dm³ outside STP conditions</li>
  <li>Forgetting to divide when finding moles</li>
  <li>Mixing up mass and volume</li>
  <li>Ignoring units</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> QUICK SUMMARY</h3>
<pre>
STP GAS RULE:
Volume = moles × 22.4
Moles = volume ÷ 22.4
</pre>
', 1),
  (137, 137, '<h2>Importance and Applications of the Mole Concept</h2>
<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>The mole concept is a bridge between the microscopic world (atoms, molecules, ions) and the macroscopic world (grams, liters).</li>
<li>It allows chemists to count particles indirectly using mass and volume.</li>
<li>It is essential in chemical equations because reactions happen in mole ratios.</li>
<li>It helps in predicting amounts of reactants and products.</li>
<li>It is used in industries, laboratories, and daily chemical production.</li>
</ul>

<h3> KEY IMPORTANCE POINTS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Converts mass ↔ moles ↔ particles</li>
<li>Helps balance and interpret chemical equations</li>
<li>Used in gas volume calculations</li>
<li>Used in solution concentration (molarity)</li>
<li>Helps in industrial yield prediction</li>
</ul>
<h3> REAL-LIFE APPLICATION AREAS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Pharmaceutical industry (drug dosage calculations)</li>
<li>Food industry (ingredient proportions)</li>
<li>Fertilizer production (NPK ratios)</li>
<li>Gas industry (oxygen, ammonia, LPG production)</li>
<li>Environmental chemistry (pollution measurement)</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (138, 138, '<h2>Balancing Chemical Equations</h2>

<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>A chemical equation must show the same number of atoms on both reactant and product sides.</li>
<li>This is based on the Law of Conservation of Mass.</li>
<li>Atoms are not created or destroyed during a chemical reaction.</li>
<li>Only coefficients (numbers in front) are changed, not subscripts.</li>
<li>Balanced equations represent real chemical proportions.</li>
</ul>

<h3> KEY RULES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Never change the formula of a compound</li>
<li>Only adjust coefficients</li>
<li>Balance one element at a time</li>
<li>Check final atom count on both sides</li>
</ul>

<h3> SIMPLE MODEL</h3>
<pre>
Unbalanced:   H₂ + O₂ → H₂O
Balanced:     2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O
</pre>

<h3> WHY BALANCING IS IMPORTANT</h3>
<ul>
<li>Ensures conservation of mass</li>
<li>Shows correct mole ratios</li>
<li>Needed for stoichiometric calculations</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (139, 139, '<h2>Law of Conservation of Mass</h2>

<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Law of Conservation of Mass states that mass is neither created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction.</li>
<li>The total mass of reactants is always equal to the total mass of products.</li>
<li>During a reaction, atoms are only rearranged, not lost or created.</li>
<li>This law is the foundation of balancing chemical equations.</li>
</ul>

<h3> KEY IDEA (ATOMIC LEVEL)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Atoms are conserved → only bonds break and form</li>
<li>No change in number of atoms → same total mass</li>
</ul>

<h3> SIMPLE MODEL</h3>
<pre>
Reactants → rearrangement of atoms → Products
Mass before = Mass after
</pre>

<h3> REAL EXPERIMENT IDEA</h3>
<ul>
<li>If a reaction is done in a closed container, total mass stays the same.</li>
<li>If done in an open container, gas may escape → apparent mass loss.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (140, 140, '<h2>Chemical Equations</h2>
<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>A chemical equation is a symbolic representation of a chemical reaction.</li>
<li>It shows reactants on the left and products on the right.</li>
<li>Arrows indicate the direction of the reaction.</li>
<li>Coefficients are used to balance equations.</li>
<li>Chemical equations must obey the Law of Conservation of Mass.</li>
</ul>

<h3> KEY TERMS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Reactants:</b> Starting substances in a reaction</li>
<li><b>Products:</b> New substances formed after reaction</li>
<li><b>Arrow (→):</b> Means “yields” or “produces”</li>
</ul>

<h3> SIMPLE MODEL</h3>
<pre>
Reactants  →  Products
Mg + O₂     →  MgO
</pre>

<h3> EXAMPLE WITH BALANCING</h3>
<pre>
Unbalanced: Mg + O₂ → MgO
Balanced:   2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO
</pre>
', 1),
  (141, 141, '<h2>Neutralization</h2>

<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Neutralization is a chemical reaction between an acid and a base.</li>
<li>It produces salt and water only.</li>
<li>It is an exothermic reaction (releases heat).</li>
<li>H⁺ ions from acid combine with OH⁻ ions from base to form water.</li>
</ul>

<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<ul>
<li>Acid + Base → Salt + Water</li>
</ul>

<h3> SIMPLE MODEL</h3>
<pre>
HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O
(acid) (base)   (salt) (water)
</pre>

<h3> IONIC VIEW</h3>
<pre>
H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O
</pre>

<h3> CHARACTERISTICS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Temperature increases (heat released)</li>
<li>pH moves towards 7 (neutral)</li>
<li>Salt is formed</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (142, 142, '<h2>Acids</h2>
<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Acids are substances that produce hydrogen ions (H⁺) when dissolved in water.</li>
<li>They are usually corrosive and can burn tissues.</li>
<li>Acids turn blue litmus paper red.</li>
<li>They have a sour taste (but should never be tested in a lab).</li>
<li>Acids can be strong or weak depending on the amount of ionization in water.</li>
</ul>

<h3> ARRHENIUS DEFINITION</h3>
<ul>
<li>An acid is a substance that increases the concentration of H⁺ ions in aqueous solution.</li>
</ul>

<h3> DISSOCIATION MODEL</h3>
<pre>
HCl → H⁺ + Cl⁻
H₂SO₄ → 2H⁺ + SO₄²⁻
CH₃COOH ⇌ H⁺ + CH₃COO⁻ (weak acid)
</pre>

<h3> COMMON ACIDS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Hydrochloric acid (HCl)</li>
<li>Sulfuric acid (H₂SO₄)</li>
<li>Nitric acid (HNO₃)</li>
<li>Acetic acid (CH₃COOH)</li>
</ul>

<h3> INDICATOR EFFECTS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Blue litmus → Red</li>
<li>Methyl orange → Red</li>
<li>Phenolphthalein → Colourless</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (143, 143, '<h2>Bases & Alkalis</h2>
<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Bases are substances that neutralize acids by reacting with them.</li>
<li>Alkalis are bases that are soluble in water.</li>
<li>When dissolved in water, alkalis produce hydroxide ions (OH⁻).</li>
<li>Bases are usually bitter and feel slippery (DO NOT taste in lab).</li>
<li>Alkalis are a special group of bases.</li>
</ul>

<h3> ARRHENIUS DEFINITION</h3>
<ul>
<li>A base is a substance that increases the concentration of OH⁻ ions in water.</li>
</ul>

<h3> DISSOCIATION MODEL</h3>
<pre>
NaOH → Na⁺ + OH⁻
KOH → K⁺ + OH⁻
Ca(OH)₂ → Ca²⁺ + 2OH⁻
</pre>

<h3> COMMON BASES & ALKALIS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Sodium hydroxide (NaOH)</li>
<li>Potassium hydroxide (KOH)</li>
<li>Calcium hydroxide (Ca(OH)₂)</li>
<li>Ammonia solution (NH₃ in water)</li>
</ul>

<h3> INDICATOR EFFECTS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Red litmus → Blue</li>
<li>Phenolphthalein → Pink</li>
<li>Methyl orange → Yellow</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (144, 144, '<h2>Indicators</h2>
<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Indicators are substances that show whether a solution is acidic, basic, or neutral by changing colour.</li>
<li>They work because they change structure in acidic or basic conditions.</li>
<li>They help scientists avoid tasting or guessing chemicals in the lab.</li>
</ul>

<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<ul>
<li>Acid → High H⁺ concentration</li>
<li>Base → High OH⁻ concentration</li>
<li>Indicator responds to these ions by changing colour</li>
</ul>

<h3> COMMON INDICATORS AND COLOURS</h3>
<pre>
Litmus:
Acid → Red
Base → Blue

Phenolphthalein:
Acid → Colourless
Base → Pink

Methyl orange:
Acid → Red
Base → Yellow
</pre>

<h3> FUNCTION OF INDICATORS</h3>
<ul>
<li>To test acidity or alkalinity</li>
<li>To identify unknown substances</li>
<li>To monitor neutralization reactions</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (145, 145, '<h2>pH Scale</h2>

<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>The pH scale is used to measure how acidic or basic a solution is.</li>
<li>It is based on the concentration of hydrogen ions (H⁺) in a solution.</li>
<li>The scale ranges from 0 to 14.</li>
<li>pH = 7 is neutral (equal H⁺ and OH⁻ ions).</li>
<li>pH less than 7 is acidic (more H⁺ ions).</li>
<li>pH greater than 7 is basic (more OH⁻ ions).</li>
</ul>

<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<ul>
<li>Low pH → high H⁺ concentration → acidic</li>
<li>High pH → low H⁺ concentration → basic</li>
</ul>

<h3> pH SCALE MODEL</h3>
<pre>
0 —— 1 —— 3 —— 7 —— 10 —— 14
Strong acid   Neutral   Strong base
</pre>

<h3> TYPICAL pH RANGES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Strong acids: 0–3</li>
<li>Weak acids: 4–6</li>
<li>Neutral: 7</li>
<li>Weak bases: 8–10</li>
<li>Strong bases: 11–14</li>
</ul>

<h3> REAL-LIFE APPLICATIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Soil testing in agriculture</li>
<li>Blood pH regulation in the human body (~7.4)</li>
<li>Water quality testing</li>
<li>Swimming pool maintenance</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (146, 146, '<h2>Salts</h2>
<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Salts are ionic compounds formed when the hydrogen ion (H⁺) of an acid is replaced by a metal ion or ammonium ion.</li>
<li>They are produced during neutralization reactions.</li>
<li>Salts are made of positive and negative ions held together by ionic bonds.</li>
<li>Most salts are crystalline solids.</li>
</ul>

<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<ul>
<li>Acid + Base → Salt + Water</li>
<li>H⁺ is replaced by metal ion (e.g. Na⁺, K⁺, Ca²⁺)</li>
</ul>

<h3> EXAMPLE</h3>
<pre>
HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O
Salt formed: NaCl (sodium chloride)
</pre>

<h3> TYPES OF SALTS (INTRO)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Normal salts (NaCl, KNO₃)</li>
<li>Acid salts (NaHSO₄)</li>
<li>Basic salts (Cu(OH)Cl)</li>
</ul>

<h3> GENERAL FORMATION IDEA</h3>
<ul>
<li>Acid provides anion (Cl⁻, SO₄²⁻, NO₃⁻)</li>
<li>Base provides cation (Na⁺, K⁺, Ca²⁺)</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (147, 147, '<h2>Applications of Acids & Bases</h2>
<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Acids and bases are widely used in daily life, industry, and agriculture.</li>
<li>Their chemical reactivity makes them useful in production, cleaning, and energy systems.</li>
<li>Acids are used when controlled reactions are needed (e.g. batteries, fertilizers).</li>
<li>Bases are used for neutralization, cleaning, and manufacturing soaps.</li>
</ul>

<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<ul>
<li>Acids → provide H⁺ ions → used in energy and industrial reactions</li>
<li>Bases → provide OH⁻ ions → used in neutralization and cleaning</li>
</ul>

<h3> SIMPLE SUMMARY</h3>
<pre>
Acids → Batteries, Fertilizers, Food preservation
Bases → Soap, Cleaning agents, Antacids
</pre>

<h3> INDUSTRIAL & DAILY APPLICATIONS</h3>

<h4>Acids</h4>
<ul>
<li>Sulfuric acid → car batteries</li>
<li>Hydrochloric acid → cleaning metals</li>
<li>Nitric acid → fertilizers (ammonium nitrate)</li>
<li>Carbonic acid → soft drinks</li>
</ul>

<h4>Bases</h4>
<ul>
<li>Sodium hydroxide → soap making</li>
<li>Calcium hydroxide → neutralizing acidic soil</li>
<li>Magnesium hydroxide → antacids (stomach relief)</li>
<li>Ammonia solution → cleaning agents</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (148, 148, '<h2>Strength of Acids & Bases</h2>
<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>The strength of an acid or base depends on the degree of ionization in water.</li>
<li>Strong acids and bases fully dissociate into ions in solution.</li>
<li>Weak acids and bases only partially dissociate in water.</li>
<li>The more ions produced, the stronger the acid or base.</li>
</ul>

<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<ul>
<li>Strength ≠ concentration</li>
<li>Strength depends on ionization, not amount of substance</li>
</ul>

<h3> IONIZATION MODEL</h3>
<pre>
Strong acid:
HCl → H⁺ + Cl⁻   (complete ionization)

Weak acid:
CH₃COOH ⇌ H⁺ + CH₃COO⁻   (partial ionization)

Strong base:
NaOH → Na⁺ + OH⁻

Weak base:
NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻
</pre>

<h3> CONDUCTIVITY IDEA</h3>
<ul>
<li>Strong acids → many ions → high conductivity</li>
<li>Weak acids → few ions → low conductivity</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (149, 149, '<h2>Metals</h2>
<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Metals are elements that tend to lose electrons and form positive ions (cations).</li>
<li>They are good conductors of heat and electricity due to free (delocalized) electrons.</li>
<li>They are malleable (can be hammered into sheets).</li>
<li>They are ductile (can be drawn into wires).</li>
<li>They usually have high melting and boiling points (except a few like mercury).</li>
<li>They have a shiny surface (metallic lustre).</li>
</ul>

<h3> ELECTRON SEA MODEL</h3>
<ul>
<li>Metal atoms release outer electrons.</li>
<li>These electrons move freely through the structure.</li>
<li>This “sea of electrons” allows conductivity and bonding strength.</li>
</ul>

<h3> STRUCTURE MODEL</h3>
<pre>
Metal lattice:
[+][+][+][+]
 e⁻ e⁻ e⁻ e⁻   → free electrons flow
[+][+][+][+]
</pre>

<h3> KEY PROPERTIES SUMMARY</h3>
<ul>
<li>Conduct electricity → free electrons carry charge</li>
<li>Conduct heat → energy transferred through electrons</li>
<li>Malleable → layers of atoms slide without breaking bonds</li>
<li>Ductile → atoms rearrange under force</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (150, 150, '<h2>Non-metals</h2>

<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Non-metals are elements that tend to gain or share electrons during chemical reactions.</li>
<li>They form negative ions (anions) when they gain electrons.</li>
<li>They are poor conductors of heat and electricity due to lack of free electrons.</li>
<li>Most non-metals are brittle in solid state and break easily.</li>
<li>They can exist in all three states: solid, liquid (bromine), and gas.</li>
</ul>

<h3> ELECTRON BEHAVIOR</h3>
<ul>
<li>Non-metals have 4–8 electrons in outer shell.</li>
<li>They gain electrons to achieve stable configuration.</li>
<li>They form covalent bonds by sharing electrons or ionic bonds by gaining electrons.</li>
</ul>

<h3> STRUCTURE MODEL</h3>
<pre>
Non-metal solid:
X   X    X
  X   X
(no free electrons → no conductivity)
</pre>

<h3> KEY PROPERTIES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Poor conductors of electricity and heat</li>
<li>Brittle (break easily when struck)</li>
<li>No metallic lustre (dull appearance)</li>
<li>Low melting and boiling points (most of them)</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (151, 151, '<h2>Reactivity Series</h2>
<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>The reactivity series is an arrangement of metals in order of how easily they lose electrons.</li>
<li>Metals at the top lose electrons easily → highly reactive.</li>
<li>Metals at the bottom lose electrons with difficulty → less reactive.</li>
<li>Hydrogen is included for comparison in displacement reactions.</li>
</ul>

<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<ul>
<li>More reactive metals lose electrons faster and form ions easily.</li>
</ul>

<h3> REACTIVITY SERIES</h3>
<pre>
K > Na > Ca > Mg > Al > Zn > Fe > H > Cu > Ag > Au
(Highly reactive → Least reactive)
</pre>

<h3> IMPORTANT CONSEQUENCES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Top metals react vigorously with water and acids</li>
<li>Bottom metals are less reactive and often found uncombined in nature</li>
<li>More reactive metals displace less reactive metals from compounds</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (152, 152, '<h2>Displacement Reactions</h2>

<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>A displacement reaction is a reaction where a more reactive element replaces a less reactive element from its compound.</li>
<li>It depends on the reactivity series of metals.</li>
<li>Only a metal higher in the reactivity series can displace a metal lower in the series.</li>
<li>Displacement reactions involve electron transfer (redox reactions).</li>
</ul>

<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<ul>
<li>More reactive metal loses electrons (oxidation).</li>
<li>Less reactive metal ion gains electrons (reduction).</li>
<li>Thus, displacement reactions are redox reactions.</li>
</ul>

<h3> STANDARD EXAMPLE</h3>
<pre>
Zn + CuSO₄ → ZnSO₄ + Cu

Zinc (Zn) is more reactive than copper (Cu),
so Zn displaces Cu from copper sulfate solution.
</pre>

<h3> REDOX IDENTIFICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Oxidation: Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻</li>
<li>Reduction: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu</li>
</ul>

<ul>
<li>Reducing agent: Zinc (Zn)</li>
<li>Oxidising agent: Cu²⁺ ions</li>
</ul>

<h3> OBSERVATIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Blue copper sulfate solution fades</li>
<li>Brown copper metal forms as a solid deposit</li>
<li>Zinc metal gradually dissolves</li>
</ul>

<h3> MINI CALCULATION EXAMPLE</h3>
<pre>
If 2 moles of Zn react completely:

Zn : Cu ratio = 1 : 1

2 mol Zn → 2 mol Cu produced
</pre>
', 1),
  (153, 153, '<h2>Corrosion (Rusting)</h2>
<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Corrosion is the gradual destruction of metals due to chemical reaction with the environment.</li>
<li>Rusting is a specific type of corrosion that affects iron and steel.</li>
<li>It occurs when iron reacts with oxygen and water to form hydrated iron(III) oxide (rust).</li>
<li>Rusting is a redox process involving oxidation of iron.</li>
</ul>

<h3> CONDITIONS FOR RUSTING</h3>
<ul>
<li>Oxygen (air)</li>
<li>Water (moisture)</li>
<li>Electrolytes (speed up rusting, e.g. salt water)</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> SIMPLE EXAMPLE (HOW x IS FOUND)</h3>
<pre>
If 1 mole of rust weighs 178 g
Fe₂O₃ = 160 g
H₂O = 18 g each
</pre>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Subtract iron oxide part</p>
<pre>
178 - 160 = 18 g
</pre>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Find water amount</p>
<pre>
18 g ÷ 18 g/mol = 1 mole of water
</pre>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> So x = 1</p>
<hr>
<h3> FINAL ANSWER</h3>
<pre>
x = 1
Final formula:
Fe₂O₃·H₂O
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> MEMORY TRICK</h3>
<p>
Rust = Iron + Air + Water → weak brown layer that keeps growing
</p>
<h3> OBSERVATIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Iron turns brown</li>
<li>Surface becomes rough</li>
<li>Metal becomes weak over time</li>
</ul>
<h3> PREVENTION METHODS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Painting → blocks air and water</li>
<li>Oiling/greasing → prevents moisture contact</li>
<li>Galvanizing → coating iron with zinc</li>
<li>Alloying → making stainless steel (iron + chromium)</li>
<li>Plastic coating → physical barrier</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (154, 154, '<h2>Extraction of Metals</h2>

<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Metals are obtained from naturally occurring rocks called ores.</li>
<li>An ore is a compound of a metal mixed with impurities (gangue).</li>
<li>The method used to extract a metal depends on its position in the reactivity series.</li>
</ul>

<h3> EXTRACTION PRINCIPLE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Highly reactive metals → extracted by electrolysis</li>
<li>Moderately reactive metals → extracted by carbon reduction</li>
<li>Unreactive metals → found in native state (no extraction needed)</li>
</ul>

<h3> SIMPLIFIED FLOW</h3>
<pre>
Ore → Concentration → Extraction → Purification → Pure Metal
</pre>

<h3> METHODS OF EXTRACTION</h3>

<h4>1. Electrolysis (Very reactive metals)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Used for: Potassium, Sodium, Aluminum</li>
<li>Uses electric current to break compounds</li>
</ul>

<h4>2. Carbon reduction (Moderately reactive metals)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Used for: Iron, Zinc, Lead</li>
<li>Carbon removes oxygen from ore</li>
</ul>

<h4>3. Native extraction (Low reactivity metals)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Used for: Gold, Silver, Platinum</li>
<li>Metals are found uncombined in nature</li>
</ul>

<h3> BLAST FURNACE (IRON EXTRACTION)</h3>
<pre>
Iron ore + Coke + Limestone → Iron + Slag
</pre>

<h3> ROLE OF MATERIALS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Coke → reduces iron oxide to iron</li>
<li>Limestone → removes impurities (forms slag)</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (155, 155, '<h2>Alloys</h2>

<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>An alloy is a mixture of two or more elements, at least one of which is a metal.</li>
<li>Alloys are designed to improve properties of pure metals.</li>
<li>They are generally harder, stronger, and more resistant to corrosion than pure metals.</li>
</ul>

<h3> WHY ALLOYS ARE STRONGER</h3>
<ul>
<li>Pure metals have regular layers of atoms that slide easily.</li>
<li>In alloys, different-sized atoms distort the layers.</li>
<li>This distortion prevents layers from sliding easily → increases strength.</li>
</ul>

<h3> STRUCTURE IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Pure metal:   layers slide easily → soft

Alloy:   mixed atoms distort layers → hard
</pre>

<h3> TYPES OF ALLOYS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Substitutional alloy → atoms of similar size replace each other (e.g. brass)</li>
<li>Interstitial alloy → small atoms fit into gaps (e.g. steel)</li>
</ul>

<h3> COMMON EXAMPLES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Steel = Iron + Carbon</li>
<li>Brass = Copper + Zinc</li>
<li>Bronze = Copper + Tin</li>
<li>Solder = Lead + Tin</li>
</ul>

<h3> USES OF ALLOYS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Construction (steel bridges, buildings)</li>
<li>Tools and machinery</li>
<li>Electrical components</li>
<li>Coins and decorative items</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (156, 156, '<h2>Uses of Metals</h2>
<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Metals are widely used in daily life because of their physical and chemical properties.</li>
<li>Each metal is chosen for a specific use based on its properties such as strength, conductivity, and resistance to corrosion.</li>
</ul>

<h3> PROPERTY → USE LINK</h3>
<ul>
<li>Good conductors → electrical wiring</li>
<li>Strong metals → construction</li>
<li>Unreactive metals → jewelry and decoration</li>
<li>Malleable metals → shaping into sheets and objects</li>
</ul>

<h3> COMMON USES</h3>
<pre>
Iron → buildings, bridges, machinery
Copper → electrical wiring, cables
Aluminium → aircraft, utensils, packaging
Gold → jewelry, electronics
Silver → ornaments, photography
</pre>

<h3> REASONING IDEA</h3>
<ul>
<li>We choose metals based on their properties, not randomly.</li>
<li>Each property makes a metal suitable for a specific application.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (157, 157, '<h2>Introduction: Air, Oxygen & Water</h2>
<h3> CORE IDEA</h3>
<ul>
<li>Air, oxygen, and water are essential substances for life on Earth</li>
<li>They support biological, chemical, and environmental processes</li>
<li>This topic explains their composition, properties, uses, and reactions</li>
</ul>

<h3> KEY UNDERSTANDING</h3>
<p>
Air is not a single gas — it is a <b>mixture of gases</b> mainly nitrogen and oxygen.
Oxygen is a <b>reactive gas</b> that supports respiration and combustion.
Water is a <b>universal solvent</b> that dissolves many substances and enables reactions.
</p>

<h3> COMPOSITION OF AIR</h3>
<ul>
<li>Nitrogen (≈78%) → reduces rate of combustion</li>
<li>Oxygen (≈21%) → supports respiration and burning</li>
<li>Carbon dioxide (≈0.04%) → used by plants in photosynthesis</li>
<li>Other gases (argon, water vapour) → present in small amounts</li>
</ul>

<h3> SIMPLE RELATIONSHIP MAP</h3>
<pre>
Air → provides oxygen
Oxygen → supports respiration & combustion
Water → supports life & dissolves substances
</pre>

<h3> WHY THIS TOPIC MATTERS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Explains how life is supported on Earth</li>
<li>Helps understand combustion and rusting</li>
<li>Explains environmental processes like pollution</li>
<li>Forms the foundation for studying gases and solutions</li>
</ul>

<h3> KEY PROPERTIES</h3>

<h4>Oxygen</h4>
<ul>
<li>Colorless and odorless gas</li>
<li>Supports combustion but does not burn</li>
<li>Essential for respiration</li>
</ul>

<h4>Water</h4>
<ul>
<li>Exists in solid, liquid, and gas states</li>
<li>Neutral (pH ≈ 7)</li>
<li>Dissolves many substances</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (158, 158, '<h2>Organic Chemistry</h2>

<h3> FOUNDATION NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Organic chemistry is the study of carbon-containing compounds.</li>
<li>Carbon is special because it forms <strong>4 covalent bonds</strong>.</li>
<li>It shows <strong>catenation</strong> → ability to form long chains and rings.</li>
<li>Main groups include: <strong>hydrocarbons</strong> (only C and H).</li>
</ul>

<h3> CARBON STRUCTURE IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Straight chain:   C–C–C–C–C
Branched chain:       C
                     / | \
                    C  C  C
</pre>

<h3> TYPES OF HYDROCARBONS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Alkanes</b> → single bonds (C–C)</li>
<li><b>Alkenes</b> → at least one double bond (C=C)</li>
<li><b>Alkynes</b> → at least one triple bond (C≡C)</li>
</ul>

<h3> IMPORTANT FORMULAS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Alkanes: CₙH₂ₙ₊₂</li>
<li>Alkenes: CₙH₂ₙ</li>
<li>Alkynes: CₙH₂ₙ₋₂</li>
</ul>

<h3> EXAMPLE REACTIONS</h3>

<h4>1. Combustion of methane</h4>
<pre>
CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O
</pre>
<p><b>Step-by-step:</b></p>
<ul>
<li>Carbon reacts with oxygen → carbon dioxide</li>
<li>Hydrogen reacts with oxygen → water</li>
<li>Energy is released (exothermic reaction)</li>
</ul>

<h4>2. Formation of ethane (catenation)</h4>
<pre>
CH₃ + CH₃ → C₂H₆
</pre>
<ul>
<li>Two carbon units join to form a longer chain</li>
<li>This shows carbon bonding ability</li>
</ul>

<h4>3. Ethene addition reaction</h4>
<pre>
C₂H₄ + H₂ → C₂H₆
</pre>
<ul>
<li>Double bond breaks</li>
<li>Hydrogen atoms add to carbon</li>
<li>Product becomes saturated alkane</li>
</ul>

<h4>4. Complete combustion of ethane</h4>
<pre>
2C₂H₆ + 7O₂ → 4CO₂ + 6H₂O
</pre>

</div>', 1),
  (159, 159, '<h2>Alkanes</h2>

<h3> FOUNDATION NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Alkanes are <b>saturated hydrocarbons</b>.</li>
<li>They contain only <b>single covalent bonds (C–C)</b>.</li>
<li>They are generally <b>less reactive</b> than alkenes and alkynes.</li>
<li>General formula: <b>CₙH₂ₙ₊₂</b></li>
<li>Each carbon forms 4 bonds (tetravalency).</li>
</ul>

<h3> STRUCTURAL REPRESENTATION</h3>
<pre>
Methane:   CH₄

Ethane:    CH₃–CH₃

Propane:   CH₃–CH₂–CH₃

Butane:    CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃
</pre>

<h3> MOLECULAR FORMULAS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Methane → CH₄</li>
<li>Ethane → C₂H₆</li>
<li>Propane → C₃H₈</li>
<li>Butane → C₄H₁₀</li>
</ul>

<h3> KEY PROPERTIES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Burn in oxygen to produce CO₂ and H₂O (combustion)</li>
<li>Relatively unreactive due to strong C–C bonds</li>
<li>Non-polar molecules → insoluble in water</li>
<li>Used as fuels (e.g., methane in natural gas)</li>
</ul>

<h3> IMPORTANT REACTION</h3>

<h4>1. Combustion of methane</h4>
<pre>
CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O
</pre>

<p><b>Step-by-step:</b></p>
<ul>
<li>Step 1: Carbon reacts with oxygen → CO₂</li>
<li>Step 2: Hydrogen reacts with oxygen → H₂O</li>
<li>Step 3: Energy is released (exothermic reaction)</li>
</ul>

<h4>2. Combustion of propane</h4>
<pre>
C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O
</pre>

<h3> STRUCTURE IDEA (CHAIN GROWTH)</h3>
<pre>
CH₄ → CH₃–CH₃ → CH₃–CH₂–CH₃ → longer chains
</pre>

<hr>
<h2>Alkenes</h2>

<h3> FOUNDATION NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Alkenes are <b>unsaturated hydrocarbons</b>.</li>
<li>They contain at least one <b>carbon-carbon double bond (C=C)</b>.</li>
<li>General formula: <b>CₙH₂ₙ</b></li>
<li>More reactive than alkanes due to the double bond.</li>
<li>They undergo <b>addition reactions</b>.</li>
</ul>

<h3> STRUCTURAL REPRESENTATION</h3>
<pre>
Ethene:    H2C=CH2

Propene:   CH3–CH=CH2

Butene:    CH3–CH2–CH=CH2
</pre>

<h3> DOUBLE BOND EXPLANATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Double bond = 1 sigma bond + 1 pi bond</li>
<li>Pi bond is weaker → breaks easily</li>
<li>This makes alkenes highly reactive</li>
</ul>

<h3> IMPORTANT REACTIONS</h3>

<h4>1. Hydrogenation (Addition of Hydrogen)</h4>
<pre>
C₂H₄ + H₂ → C₂H₆
</pre>

<p><b>Step-by-step:</b></p>
<ul>
<li>Step 1: Identify double bond (C=C)</li>
<li>Step 2: Break pi bond</li>
<li>Step 3: Add hydrogen atoms</li>
<li>Step 4: Product becomes alkane</li>
</ul>

<h4>2. Addition of Bromine (Test for alkenes)</h4>
<pre>
C₂H₄ + Br₂ → C₂H₄Br₂
</pre>

<p><b>Observation:</b> Brown bromine water turns colorless</p>

<h4>3. Combustion of ethene</h4>
<pre>
C₂H₄ + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 2H₂O
</pre>

<h3> COMPARISON: ALKANES vs ALKENES</h3>
<pre>
Alkanes: C–C (single bond), less reactive
Alkenes: C=C (double bond), more reactive
</pre>
</div>
<hr>
<h2>Alkynes</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Alkynes are <b>unsaturated hydrocarbons</b>.</li>
<li>They contain at least one <b>carbon-carbon triple bond (C≡C)</b>.</li>
<li>General formula: <b>CₙH₂ₙ₋₂</b></li>
<li>They are more reactive than alkanes and alkenes.</li>
<li>They undergo <b>addition reactions</b> due to the triple bond.</li>
</ul>

<h3> STRUCTURAL REPRESENTATION</h3>
<pre>
Ethyne:    HC≡CH

Propyne:   CH₃–C≡CH

Butyne:    CH₃–CH₂–C≡CH
</pre>

<h3> TRIPLE BOND EXPLANATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Triple bond = 1 sigma bond + 2 pi bonds</li>
<li>Pi bonds are weak and easily broken</li>
<li>This makes alkynes highly reactive</li>
</ul>

<h3> IMPORTANT REACTIONS</h3>

<h4>1. Hydrogenation (Addition of Hydrogen)</h4>
<pre>
C₂H₂ + 2H₂ → C₂H₆
</pre>

<p><b>Step-by-step:</b></p>
<ul>
<li>Step 1: Identify triple bond (C≡C)</li>
<li>Step 2: Break pi bonds</li>
<li>Step 3: Add hydrogen atoms stepwise</li>
<li>Step 4: Product becomes alkane</li>
</ul>

<h4>2. Addition of Bromine</h4>
<pre>
C₂H₂ + Br₂ → C₂H₂Br₂ (first addition)
C₂H₂Br₂ + Br₂ → C₂H₂Br₄ (further addition)
</pre>

<p><b>Observation:</b> Bromine water is decolorized</p>

<h4>3. Combustion of ethyne</h4>
<pre>
2C₂H₂ + 5O₂ → 4CO₂ + 2H₂O
</pre>

<h3> COMPARISON: ALKANES vs ALKENES vs ALKYNES</h3>
<pre>
Alkanes: C–C (single bond) → least reactive
Alkenes: C=C (double bond) → more reactive
Alkynes: C≡C (triple bond) → most reactive
</pre>
', 1),
  (160, 160, '<h2>Combustion of Fuels</h2>

<h3> FOUNDATION NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Combustion is the <b>burning of a fuel in oxygen</b>.</li>
<li>Most fuels are <b>hydrocarbons</b>.</li>
<li>Complete combustion produces <b>carbon dioxide (CO₂) and water (H₂O)</b>.</li>
<li>Energy is released in the form of <b>heat and light</b> (exothermic reaction).</li>
</ul>

<h3> GENERAL EQUATION</h3>
<pre>
Hydrocarbon + Oxygen → Carbon dioxide + Water + Energy
</pre>

<h3> EXAMPLE COMBUSTION REACTIONS</h3>

<h4>1. Methane combustion</h4>
<pre>
CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O + energy
</pre>

<h4>2. Ethane combustion</h4>
<pre>
2C₂H₆ + 7O₂ → 4CO₂ + 6H₂O + energy
</pre>

<h4>3. Propane combustion</h4>
<pre>
C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O + energy
</pre>

<h3> TYPES OF COMBUSTION</h3>

<h4>1. Complete Combustion</h4>
<ul>
<li>Enough oxygen available</li>
<li>Produces CO₂ and H₂O</li>
<li>Clean flame (blue flame)</li>
</ul>

<h4>2. Incomplete Combustion</h4>
<ul>
<li>Limited oxygen supply</li>
<li>Produces CO or carbon (soot)</li>
<li>Produces less energy</li>
</ul>

<pre>
CH₄ + O₂ → CO + H₂O + energy (incomplete)
</pre>

<h3> ENERGY EXPLANATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Energy is needed to break old bonds (C–H and O=O)</li>
<li>Energy is released when new bonds form (C=O and O–H)</li>
<li>If released energy > absorbed energy → exothermic reaction</li>
</ul>

<h3> COMPARISON TABLE</h3>
<pre>
Complete combustion → CO₂ + H₂O → more energy
Incomplete combustion → CO / C + H₂O → less energy
</pre>
</div>', 1),
  (161, 161, '<h2>Alcohols (Alkanols)</h2>

<h3> FOUNDATION NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Alcohols are organic compounds containing the <b>hydroxyl group (–OH)</b>.</li>
<li>They belong to a homologous series called <b>alkanols</b>.</li>
<li>General formula: <b>CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH</b></li>
<li>They are formed when a hydrogen atom in an alkane is replaced by –OH.</li>
</ul>

<h3> STRUCTURAL FORMULAS</h3>
<pre>
Methanol:   CH₃OH
Ethanol:    CH₃CH₂OH
Propanol:   CH₃CH₂CH₂OH
</pre>

<h3> FUNCTIONAL GROUP</h3>
<ul>
<li>Functional group = <b>–OH (hydroxyl group)</b></li>
<li>This group determines chemical properties</li>
</ul>

<h3> PREPARATION IDEA (BASIC)</h3>
<pre>
Alkane → Alcohol (substitution reaction)
</pre>

<h3> COMBUSTION OF ETHANOL</h3>
<pre>
C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O + energy
</pre>

<p><b>Step-by-step:</b></p>
<ul>
<li>Step 1: Carbon forms CO₂</li>
<li>Step 2: Hydrogen forms H₂O</li>
<li>Step 3: Energy is released</li>
</ul>

<h3> OXIDATION OF ALCOHOLS</h3>

<h4>1. Ethanol oxidation</h4>
<pre>
C₂H₅OH + [O] → CH₃CHO + H₂O
(ethanol → ethanal)
</pre>

<h4>2. Further oxidation</h4>
<pre>
CH₃CHO + [O] → CH₃COOH
(ethanal → ethanoic acid)
</pre>

<h3> KEY PROPERTIES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Burn easily (flammable)</li>
<li>Soluble in water (short-chain alcohols)</li>
<li>Used in fuels and drinks (ethanol)</li>
</ul>

<h3> IMPORTANT IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Alcohol → Aldehyde → Carboxylic Acid (oxidation pathway)
</pre>
</div>', 1),
  (162, 162, '<h2>Carboxylic Acids (Organic Acids)</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Carboxylic acids are organic compounds containing the <b>carboxyl group (–COOH)</b>.</li>
<li>They are called <b>organic acids</b> because they release hydrogen ions (H⁺) in solution.</li>
<li>They are weak acids (do not fully ionize in water).</li>
<li>General formula: <b>CₙH₂ₙ₊₁COOH</b></li>
</ul>

<h3> STRUCTURAL FORMULAS</h3>
<pre>
Methanoic acid:   HCOOH
Ethanoic acid:    CH₃COOH
Propanoic acid:   CH₃CH₂COOH
Butanoic acid:    CH₃CH₂CH₂COOH
</pre>

<h3> FUNCTIONAL GROUP</h3>
<ul>
<li>Functional group = <b>–COOH (carboxyl group)</b></li>
<li>Responsible for acidic properties</li>
</ul>

<h3> FORMATION (FROM ALCOHOLS)</h3>

<h4>Oxidation of ethanol</h4>
<pre>
CH₃CH₂OH + [O] → CH₃COOH + H₂O
</pre>

<p><b>Step-by-step:</b></p>
<ul>
<li>Step 1: Ethanol is oxidized</li>
<li>Step 2: Intermediate aldehyde is formed</li>
<li>Step 3: Further oxidation gives carboxylic acid</li>
</ul>

<h3> REACTIONS OF CARBOXYLIC ACIDS</h3>

<h4>1. Reaction with metals</h4>
<pre>
2CH₃COOH + Mg → (CH₃COO)₂Mg + H₂
</pre>

<h4>2. Reaction with carbonates</h4>
<pre>
2CH₃COOH + Na₂CO₃ → 2CH₃COONa + CO₂ + H₂O
</pre>

<h3> KEY PROPERTIES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Have sour taste (vinegar = ethanoic acid)</li>
<li>Weak acids (partial ionization)</li>
<li>Conduct electricity weakly in solution</li>
<li>Form salts called <b>carboxylates</b></li>
</ul>

<h3> IMPORTANT IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Alcohol → Aldehyde → Carboxylic Acid
(oxidation pathway)
</pre>
</div>', 1),
  (163, 163, '<h2>Cracking</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Cracking is the <b>breaking of large hydrocarbon molecules into smaller, more useful molecules</b>.</li>
<li>It produces a mixture of <b>alkanes and alkenes</b>.</li>
<li>It is important in the petroleum industry.</li>
<li>It requires <b>high temperature</b> and often a <b>catalyst (zeolite)</b>.</li>
</ul>

<h3> GENERAL EQUATION</h3>
<pre>
Large Alkane → Smaller Alkane + Alkene
</pre>

<h3> EXAMPLE REACTION</h3>
<pre>
C₁₀H₂₂ → C₈H₁₈ + C₂H₄
</pre>

<p><b>Step-by-step:</b></p>
<ul>
<li>Step 1: Long-chain hydrocarbon is heated</li>
<li>Step 2: Carbon-carbon bonds break</li>
<li>Step 3: Rearrangement forms smaller molecules</li>
<li>Step 4: One product often becomes an alkene due to unsaturation</li>
</ul>

<h3> TYPES OF CRACKING</h3>

<h4>1. Thermal Cracking</h4>
<ul>
<li>Uses very high temperature (450°C–900°C)</li>
<li>High pressure</li>
<li>Produces more alkenes</li>
</ul>

<h4>2. Catalytic Cracking</h4>
<ul>
<li>Uses catalyst (zeolite or silica-alumina)</li>
<li>Lower temperature (around 450°C)</li>
<li>Produces petrol and useful fuels</li>
</ul>

<h3> WHY CRACKING IS IMPORTANT</h3>
<ul>
<li>Long hydrocarbons are less useful</li>
<li>Short hydrocarbons are better fuels (petrol, LPG)</li>
<li>Alkenes are used to make plastics (polymers)</li>
</ul>

<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Large molecule (low demand) → Small molecules (high demand fuels + plastics)
</pre>

<h3> INDUSTRIAL CONNECTION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Petroleum fractions contain long-chain alkanes</li>
<li>Cracking converts them into high-demand fuels</li>
</ul>
</div>', 1),
  (164, 164, '<h2>Isomerism</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Isomerism occurs when compounds have the <b>same molecular formula</b> but different structural arrangements.</li>
<li>These compounds are called <b>isomers</b>.</li>
<li>Isomers have different physical and sometimes chemical properties.</li>
<li>Differences arise due to <b>different atom arrangement</b>.</li>
</ul>

<h3> STRUCTURAL EXAMPLES (C₄H₁₀)</h3>

<h4>1. Butane (straight chain)</h4>
<pre>
CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃
</pre>

<h4>2. Isobutane (branched chain)</h4>
<pre>
      CH₃
       |
CH₃–CH–CH₃
</pre>

<h3> TYPES OF ISOMERISM (Basic Level)</h3>

<h4>1. Structural Isomerism</h4>
<ul>
<li>Different arrangement of atoms</li>
<li>Different carbon chain structures</li>
</ul>

<h4>2. Chain Isomerism</h4>
<ul>
<li>Different carbon skeleton (straight vs branched)</li>
<li>Example: butane vs isobutane</li>
</ul>

<h3> WHY ISOMERS HAVE DIFFERENT PROPERTIES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Different shapes affect how molecules pack together</li>
<li>Different intermolecular forces</li>
<li>This affects boiling point, melting point, density</li>
</ul>

<h3> GENERAL IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Same formula → Different structure → Different properties
</pre>
</div>', 1),
  (165, 165, '<h2>Importance of Organic Chemistry</h2>

<h3> FOUNDATION NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Organic chemistry is essential for understanding <b>carbon-based compounds</b> in daily life.</li>
<li>It plays a key role in <b>energy, industry, medicine, and materials</b>.</li>
<li>Most fuels, plastics, and biological molecules are organic compounds.</li>
</ul>

<h3> MAIN APPLICATION AREAS</h3>

<h4>1. Fuels and Energy</h4>
<ul>
<li>Hydrocarbons are used as fuels (petrol, diesel, natural gas)</li>
<li>They release energy during combustion</li>
</ul>

<pre>
CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O + energy
</pre>

<h4>2. Plastics and Polymers</h4>
<ul>
<li>Alkenes are used to make plastics (polyethene, PVC)</li>
<li>Cracking produces alkenes for polymer production</li>
</ul>

<pre>
n(CH₂=CH₂) → (–CH₂–CH₂–)ₙ  [polyethene]
</pre>

<h4>3. Medicines and Pharmaceuticals</h4>
<ul>
<li>Organic compounds are used to make drugs and antibiotics</li>
<li>Important in treating diseases</li>
</ul>

<h4>4. Everyday Products</h4>
<ul>
<li>Fuels</li>
<li>Plastics</li>
<li>Detergents</li>
<li>Fabrics (synthetic fibers)</li>
</ul>

<h3> SIMPLE FLOW IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Crude oil → hydrocarbons → fuels + plastics + chemicals
</pre>

<h3> WHY IT IS IMPORTANT</h3>
<ul>
<li>Supports modern industry</li>
<li>Provides energy for transport and electricity</li>
<li>Creates materials used in daily life</li>
</ul>

<h3> KEY SUMMARY</h3>
<pre>
Organic chemistry = foundation of fuels + materials + life-saving drugs
</pre>
</div>', 1),
  (166, 166, '<h2>Sulphur (S)</h2>

<h3> GENERAL PROPERTIES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Yellow, brittle, solid non-metal at room temperature</li>
<li>Found in volcanic regions and underground deposits</li>
<li>Has atomic number 16</li>
<li>Exists as S₈ molecules in its natural form</li>
<li>Poor conductor of heat and electricity</li>
</ul>

<h3> CHEMICAL PROPERTIES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Burns in oxygen with a blue flame</li>
<li>Forms acidic oxide sulphur dioxide (SO₂)</li>
<li>Can be further oxidized to sulphur trioxide (SO₃)</li>
</ul>

<div class="formula">
S + O₂ → SO₂  
2SO₂ + O₂ → 2SO₃  
SO₃ + H₂O → H₂SO₄
</div>

<h3> INDUSTRIAL IMPORTANCE (CONTACT PROCESS)</h3>
<ul>
<li>SO₂ is first produced by burning sulphur</li>
<li>SO₂ is converted to SO₃ using a catalyst (V₂O₅)</li>
<li>SO₃ is dissolved in water to form sulphuric acid (H₂SO₄)</li>
<li>Used conditions: high temperature (~450°C), pressure, catalyst</li>
</ul>

<h3> SULPHUR COMPOUNDS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Sulphur dioxide (SO₂):</b> toxic gas, bleaching agent, preserves food</li>
<li><b>Sulphur trioxide (SO₃):</b> reacts with water to form acid</li>
<li><b>Sulphuric acid (H₂SO₄):</b> strong acid, highly corrosive, widely used industrial acid</li>
<li><b>Hydrogen sulphide (H₂S):</b> rotten egg smell, toxic gas</li>
</ul>

<h3> USES OF SULPHURIC ACID</h3>
<ul>
<li>Manufacture of fertilizers (e.g., ammonium sulphate)</li>
<li>Car batteries (lead-acid batteries)</li>
<li>Cleaning and metal processing</li>
<li>Petroleum refining</li>
</ul>

<h3> SAFETY INFORMATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>SO₂ is harmful to lungs and causes acid rain</li>
<li>H₂S is highly poisonous even at low concentrations</li>
<li>H₂SO₄ causes severe burns on skin contact</li>
</ul>

<hr>
<h2>Sulphuric Acid (H₂SO₄)</h2>

<h3> GENERAL PROPERTIES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Strong mineral acid (one of the most important industrial acids)</li>
<li>Colourless, oily, and highly corrosive liquid</li>
<li>Very dense and heavy compared to water</li>
<li>Hygroscopic: absorbs water from air and other substances</li>
<li>Non-volatile acid (does not easily evaporate)</li>
</ul>

<h3> CHEMICAL PROPERTIES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Dissociates in water to produce H⁺ ions (strong acid behavior)</li>
<li>Strong dehydrating agent (removes water from substances)</li>
<li>Oxidizing agent when concentrated</li>
</ul>

<div class="formula">
SO₃ + H₂O → H₂SO₄
</div>

<h3> MANUFACTURE (CONTACT PROCESS SUMMARY)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Sulphur is burned to form SO₂</li>
<li>SO₂ is oxidized to SO₃ using V₂O₅ catalyst</li>
<li>SO₃ is absorbed in concentrated H₂SO₄ to form oleum</li>
<li>Oleum is diluted to form sulphuric acid</li>
</ul>

<h3> USES OF SULPHURIC ACID</h3>
<ul>
<li>Manufacture of fertilizers (ammonium sulfate, superphosphate)</li>
<li>Car batteries (lead-acid batteries)</li>
<li>Oil refining and petrochemicals</li>
<li>Metal processing and cleaning (pickling metals)</li>
<li>Manufacture of detergents, dyes, and explosives</li>
</ul>

<h3> SAFETY AND HAZARDS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Highly corrosive — causes severe skin burns</li>
<li>Strong dehydrating agent — removes water from tissues (charring effect)</li>
<li>Must always be added to water slowly (never water to acid)</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (167, 167, '<h2>Carbon (C)</h2>

<h3> GENERAL PROPERTIES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Non-metal found in all living organisms</li>
<li>Tetravalent (forms 4 covalent bonds)</li>
<li>Shows catenation (ability to form long chains and rings)</li>
<li>Exists in different allotropes: diamond, graphite, fullerenes</li>
<li>Forms a huge variety of compounds (basis of organic chemistry)</li>
</ul>

<h3> OXIDES OF CARBON</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Carbon monoxide (CO):</b> poisonous gas, incomplete combustion product</li>
<li><b>Carbon dioxide (CO₂):</b> produced during complete combustion and respiration</li>
</ul>

<div class="formula">
C + O₂ → CO₂  
2C + O₂ → 2CO
</div>

<h3> ALLOTROPES OF CARBON</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Diamond:</b> very hard, used in cutting tools, each carbon bonded to 4 others</li>
<li><b>Graphite:</b> soft, slippery, conducts electricity due to free electrons</li>
<li><b>Fullerenes:</b> spherical carbon structures used in nanotechnology</li>
</ul>

<h3> IMPORTANCE OF CARBON COMPOUNDS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Found in fuels (coal, petroleum, natural gas)</li>
<li>Basis of organic chemistry (proteins, fats, carbohydrates)</li>
<li>Used in plastics, medicines, and synthetic materials</li>
</ul>

<h3> SAFETY INFORMATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>CO is highly poisonous and binds with haemoglobin</li>
<li>CO₂ is not poisonous but can cause suffocation in high concentrations</li>
</ul>

<hr>
<h2>Carbon Allotropes: Diamond & Graphite</h2>

<h3> INTRODUCTION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Allotropes are different structural forms of the same element</li>
<li>Carbon shows allotropy due to different bonding arrangements</li>
<li>Main allotropes: Diamond, Graphite (also Fullerenes)</li>
</ul>

<h3> DIAMOND</h3>
<ul>
<li>Each carbon atom is bonded to 4 others (tetrahedral structure)</li>
<li>Forms a strong 3D giant covalent structure</li>
<li>No free electrons → does not conduct electricity</li>
<li>Very high melting point</li>
<li>Hardest natural substance</li>
</ul>

<h3> GRAPHITE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Each carbon atom bonds to 3 others forming layers</li>
<li>One free electron per carbon atom becomes delocalized</li>
<li>Layers are held by weak forces (van der Waals forces)</li>
<li>Good conductor of electricity</li>
<li>Soft and slippery texture</li>
</ul>

<h3> STRUCTURE COMPARISON</h3>
<pre>
Diamond: 3D rigid network (no free electrons)
Graphite: layered structure (free electrons move between layers)
</pre>

<h3> PROPERTIES SUMMARY</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Diamond:</b> hard, insulator, transparent, high density</li>
<li><b>Graphite:</b> soft, black, conductor, used as lubricant</li>
</ul>

<h3> USES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Diamond: cutting tools, jewelry, drilling equipment</li>
<li>Graphite: pencil leads, electrodes, lubricants</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (168, 168, '<h2>Nitrogen (N₂)</h2>

<h3> GENERAL PROPERTIES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Colorless, odorless gas</li>
<li>Makes up about 78% of Earth’s atmosphere</li>
<li>Exists mainly as diatomic molecules (N₂)</li>
<li>Non-metal and relatively inert under normal conditions</li>
</ul>

<h3> STRUCTURE AND BONDING</h3>
<ul>
<li>Two nitrogen atoms are joined by a very strong triple bond (N≡N)</li>
<li>Triple bond has very high bond energy</li>
<li>This makes nitrogen very stable and unreactive</li>
</ul>

<div class="formula">
N≡N (very strong triple covalent bond)
</div>

<h3> IMPORTANT COMPOUNDS OF NITROGEN</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Ammonia (NH₃):</b> used in fertilizers</li>
<li><b>Nitrates (NO₃⁻):</b> essential plant nutrients</li>
<li><b>Nitric acid (HNO₃):</b> used in explosives and fertilizers</li>
</ul>

<h3> NITROGEN CYCLE (BASIC IDEA)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Nitrogen gas is converted into nitrates by bacteria (nitrogen fixation)</li>
<li>Plants absorb nitrates from soil</li>
<li>Animals get nitrogen by feeding on plants</li>
<li>Decomposition returns nitrogen compounds to soil</li>
</ul>

<h3> IMPORTANCE OF NITROGEN</h3>
<ul>
<li>Essential for proteins and DNA</li>
<li>Used in fertilizers to improve crop yield</li>
<li>Helps maintain atmospheric balance</li>
</ul>

<h3> SAFETY AND LIMITATIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Nitrogen gas itself is not toxic but can cause suffocation in enclosed spaces</li>
<li>Some nitrogen compounds (like NO₂) are toxic and polluting</li>
</ul>

<hr>
<h2>Nitrogen Cycle</h2>

<h3> OVERVIEW</h3>
<ul>
<li>The nitrogen cycle is the continuous circulation of nitrogen between the atmosphere, soil, plants, and animals</li>
<li>It ensures that living organisms always have access to usable nitrogen compounds</li>
<li>Nitrogen gas (N₂) is converted into usable forms and later returned to the atmosphere</li>
</ul>

<h3> MAIN STAGES OF THE NITROGEN CYCLE</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Nitrogen fixation:</b> N₂ is converted into ammonia (NH₃) by bacteria or lightning</li>
<li><b>Nitrification:</b> NH₃ is converted into nitrites (NO₂⁻) and then nitrates (NO₃⁻)</li>
<li><b>Assimilation:</b> Plants absorb nitrates to make proteins and nucleic acids</li>
<li><b>Ammonification:</b> Decomposers break down dead organisms into ammonia</li>
<li><b>Denitrification:</b> Nitrates are converted back into nitrogen gas (N₂) by bacteria</li>
</ul>

<h3> SIMPLIFIED FLOW</h3>
<pre>
N₂ → NH₃ → NO₂⁻ → NO₃⁻ → Plants → Animals → Decomposition → N₂
</pre>

<h3> IMPORTANCE OF THE NITROGEN CYCLE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Maintains balance of nitrogen in the atmosphere</li>
<li>Provides essential nutrients for plant growth</li>
<li>Supports formation of proteins and DNA in living organisms</li>
</ul>

<h3> ROLE OF BACTERIA</h3>
<ul>
<li>Fix nitrogen from air into usable forms</li>
<li>Convert ammonia into nitrates (nitrifying bacteria)</li>
<li>Break down dead matter into ammonia</li>
<li>Convert nitrates back into nitrogen gas (denitrifying bacteria)</li>
</ul>

<h3> HUMAN IMPACT</h3>
<ul>
<li>Excess fertilizers can disrupt natural balance</li>
<li>Leads to water pollution (eutrophication)</li>
<li>Increases greenhouse gas emissions in some cases</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (169, 169, '<h2>Air Pollution</h2>

<h3> OVERVIEW</h3>
<ul>
<li>Air pollution is the contamination of air by harmful gases, smoke, and particles</li>
<li>It affects human health, climate, animals, and plants</li>
<li>Main pollutants include SO₂, CO, CO₂, NOx, and particulate matter</li>
</ul>

<h3> MAJOR AIR POLLUTANTS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Sulphur dioxide (SO₂):</b> causes acid rain and respiratory problems</li>
<li><b>Carbon monoxide (CO):</b> poisonous gas that reduces oxygen supply in blood</li>
<li><b>Carbon dioxide (CO₂):</b greenhouse gas that contributes to global warming</li>
<li><b>Nitrogen oxides (NOx):</b> contribute to smog and acid rain</li>
</ul>

<h3> CHEMICAL EFFECTS IN ENVIRONMENT</h3>
<ul>
<li>SO₂ reacts with water in clouds to form acids</li>
<li>CO binds with haemoglobin reducing oxygen transport</li>
<li>CO₂ traps infrared radiation causing greenhouse effect</li>
</ul>

<h3> KEY REACTIONS</h3>
<pre>
SO₂ + H₂O → H₂SO₄ (acid rain formation)
CO + Hb → COHb (carboxyhaemoglobin)
CO₂ → greenhouse effect (heat trapping)
</pre>

<h3> EFFECTS OF AIR POLLUTION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Respiratory diseases in humans</li>
<li>Global warming and climate change</li>
<li>Acid rain damaging buildings and crops</li>
<li>Harm to aquatic life and ecosystems</li>
</ul>

<h3> CONTROL MEASURES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Use of cleaner fuels</li>
<li>Planting trees (carbon sinks)</li>
<li>Industrial emission control (filters, scrubbers)</li>
<li>Reducing fossil fuel burning</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (170, 170, '<h2>Importance of Non-metals</h2>

<h3> OVERVIEW</h3>
<ul>
<li>Non-metals are essential elements that play key roles in life, industry, and the environment</li>
<li>They are found in air, water, living organisms, and industrial compounds</li>
<li>Without non-metals, life on Earth would not exist</li>
</ul>

<h3> IMPORTANCE IN LIVING ORGANISMS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Oxygen (O₂):</b> needed for respiration in living organisms</li>
<li><b>Nitrogen (N₂):</b> essential for proteins, DNA, and plant growth</li>
<li><b>Carbon (C):</b> backbone of all organic compounds (life molecules)</li>
<li><b>Hydrogen (H):</b> part of water and organic compounds</li>
</ul>

<h3> IMPORTANCE IN INDUSTRY</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Sulphuric acid (H₂SO₄):</b> used in fertilizers, batteries, and chemicals</li>
<li><b>Carbon dioxide (CO₂):</b> used in fire extinguishers and carbonated drinks</li>
<li><b>Chlorine (Cl₂):</b> used in water purification and disinfectants</li>
</ul>

<h3> IMPORTANCE IN AGRICULTURE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Nitrogen compounds improve soil fertility</li>
<li>Fertilizers contain nitrates, phosphates, and sulphates</li>
<li>Carbon compounds help in soil structure and organic matter</li>
</ul>

<h3> ENVIRONMENTAL IMPORTANCE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Non-metals regulate atmospheric composition</li>
<li>Carbon cycle and nitrogen cycle maintain ecosystem balance</li>
<li>CO₂ helps maintain Earth''s temperature (greenhouse effect)</li>
</ul>

<h3> BALANCE OF NON-METALS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Too much CO₂ → global warming</li>
<li>Too much SO₂ → acid rain</li>
<li>Too much CO → poisoning and pollution</li>
</ul>

<h3> SUMMARY FLOW</h3>
<pre>
Non-metals → Life processes + Industry + Agriculture + Environment balance
</pre>
', 1),
  (171, 171, '<h2>Cell Biology</h2>
<p>Cell biology is the study of cells, the basic structural and functional units of life.</p>
<h3> CELL THEORY</h3>
<pre>
1. All living organisms are made of cells
2. Cells are the basic unit of life
3. All cells come from pre-existing cells
</pre>
<h3> 1. Types of Cells</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Animal Cells</b> – no cell wall, flexible shape</li>
<li><b>Plant Cells</b> – cell wall, chloroplasts, fixed shape</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Plant Cell → Rigid + Chloroplasts
Animal Cell → Flexible + No chloroplasts
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> 2. Cell Structure (Organelles)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Nucleus</b> – controls activities, contains DNA</li>
<li><b>Cytoplasm</b> – site of reactions</li>
<li><b>Cell membrane</b> – controls entry and exit</li>
<li><b>Mitochondria</b> – energy production</li>
<li><b>Ribosomes</b> – protein synthesis</li>
<li><b>Vacuole (plant)</b> – storage</li>
<li><b>Chloroplast (plant)</b> – photosynthesis</li>
</ul>
<h3> 3. Cell Membrane & Transport</h3>
<p>The membrane controls movement of substances.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Diffusion</b> – movement from high → low concentration</li>
<li><b>Osmosis</b> – water movement across membrane</li>
<li><b>Active transport</b> – uses energy</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (172, 172, '<h2>Microscopes &amp; Cell Observation</h2>

<p>A microscope is an instrument used to magnify small objects so they can be seen clearly.</p>

<h3> 1. Functions of a Microscope</h3>
<ul>
<li>Magnifies tiny objects</li>
<li>Allows observation of cells and microorganisms</li>
<li>Helps scientists study structures not visible to the naked eye</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 2. Parts of a Light Microscope</h3>

<div style="text-align:center;margin:1.5rem 0">
<img src="/images/microscope-labelled.png" alt="Labelled diagram of a compound light microscope showing eyepiece lens, body tube, revolving nosepiece, objective lenses, stage, stage clips, diaphragm, mirror, coarse and fine adjustment knobs, arm, and base" style="max-width:100%;border-radius:12px;box-shadow:0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.15)" />
<p style="font-size:0.9em;color:#666;margin-top:0.5rem"><em>Figure 1: Labelled diagram of a compound light microscope</em></p>
</div>

<ul>
<li><b>Eyepiece Lens</b> – lens you look through</li>
<li><b>Body Tube</b> – connects eyepiece to objective lenses</li>
<li><b>Revolving Nosepiece</b> – holds and rotates objective lenses</li>
<li><b>Objective Lens</b> – magnifies the specimen (low, medium, high power)</li>
<li><b>Stage</b> – platform holding the slide</li>
<li><b>Stage Clips</b> – keep slide in place</li>
<li><b>Coarse Adjustment Knob</b> – large focusing movement</li>
<li><b>Fine Adjustment Knob</b> – sharpens image</li>
<li><b>Mirror / Illuminator</b> – provides light</li>
<li><b>Arm</b> – supports microscope body</li>
<li><b>Base</b> – supports microscope</li>
<li><b>Diaphragm</b> – controls amount of light</li>
</ul>

<pre>
Eyepiece → Viewing
Body Tube → Connects eyepiece to objectives
Revolving Nosepiece → Rotates objectives
Objective Lens → Magnification
Stage → Holds slide
Mirror → Light source
</pre>

<hr>

<h3> 3. Types of Cells Viewed Under a Microscope</h3>

<ul>
<li><b>Plant Cells</b> – rectangular shape, cell wall visible</li>
<li><b>Animal Cells</b> – irregular shape, no cell wall</li>
<li><b>Blood Cells</b> – red blood cells carry oxygen</li>
<li><b>Muscle Cells</b> – elongated cells for movement</li>
<li><b>Nerve Cells</b> – long cells for transmitting impulses</li>
<li><b>Bacteria</b> – tiny single-celled organisms</li>
<li><b>Protists</b> – organisms like amoeba and paramecium</li>
</ul>

<pre>
Plant Cell → Cell wall + Chloroplasts
Animal Cell → No cell wall
Bacteria → Very tiny unicellular organisms
</pre>

<hr>
<h3> 4. Types of Microscopes</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Light Microscope</b> – uses visible light</li>
<li><b>Electron Microscope</b> – uses electron beams</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 5. Difference Between Light and Electron Microscopes</h3>
<table border="1" cellpadding="5">
<tr>
<th>Light Microscope</th>
<th>Electron Microscope</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Uses light rays</td>
<td>Uses electron beams</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Lower magnification</td>
<td>Very high magnification</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Lower resolution</td>
<td>Higher resolution</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Can view living cells</td>
<td>Cannot view living cells</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Produces colored images</td>
<td>Produces black and white images</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Cheaper and simpler</td>
<td>Expensive and complex</td>
</tr>
</table>
<pre>
Light Microscope → Living cells
Electron Microscope → Very detailed structures
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> 6. Magnification</h3>
<p>Magnification is how many times larger an image appears compared to the real object.</p>
<h3> Formula</h3>
<pre>
Magnification = Image Size ÷ Actual Size
</pre>
<h3> Rearranged Formulae</h3>
<pre>
Image Size = Magnification × Actual Size
Actual Size = Image Size ÷ Magnification
</pre>
<h3> Example 1</h3>
<pre>
Image size = 40 mm
Actual size = 2 mm
Magnification = 40 ÷ 2
= 20×
Answer = 20×
</pre>
<h3> Example 2</h3>
<pre>
Magnification = 100×
Actual size = 0.5 mm
Image size = 100 × 0.5
= 50 mm
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> 7. Total Magnification</h3>
<p>Total magnification is found by multiplying the eyepiece lens magnification by the objective lens magnification.</p>
<pre>
Total Magnification =
Eyepiece Lens × Objective Lens
</pre>
<h3> Example</h3>
<pre>
Eyepiece = 10×
Objective = 40×
Total Magnification =
10 × 40
= 400×
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> 8. Importance of Microscopes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Help diagnose diseases</li>
<li>Used in scientific research</li>
<li>Help study microorganisms</li>
<li>Used in medicine and biology</li>
<li>Help understand cell structure</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (173, 173, '<h2>Advanced Cell Biology</h2>
<p>This section explains advanced cellular processes including cell division stages, cancer biology, stem cells, and membrane transport mechanisms.</p>
<hr>
<h3> 1. MITOSIS (CELL DIVISION IN DETAIL)</h3>
<p>Mitosis produces two identical daughter cells.</p>
<pre>
Prophase → Metaphase → Anaphase → Telophase → Cytokinesis
</pre>
<h4> Prophase</h4>
<ul>
<li>Chromosomes become visible</li>
<li>Nuclear membrane breaks down</li>
</ul>
<h4> Metaphase</h4>
<ul>
<li>Chromosomes align at equator</li>
</ul>
<h4> Anaphase</h4>
<ul>
<li>Chromatids pulled to opposite poles</li>
</ul>
<h4> Telophase</h4>
<ul>
<li>Nuclear membranes reform</li>
<li>Two nuclei form</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 2. CANCER (UNCONTROLLED MITOSIS)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Cells divide uncontrollably</li>
<li>Forms tumors</li>
<li>Caused by mutations</li>
</ul>
<h4> Causes:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Radiation</li>
<li>Chemicals</li>
<li>Genetic factors</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 3. STEM CELLS</h3>
<p>Undifferentiated cells that can become different cell types.</p>
<h4> Uses:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Repair damaged tissues</li>
<li>Treatment of diseases</li>
<li>Regenerative medicine</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 4. DIFFERENTIATION vs SPECIALIZATION</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Differentiation:</b> process of cells becoming different types</li>
<li><b>Specialization:</b> cells adapted for specific functions</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 5. CELL TRANSPORT MECHANISMS</h3>
<h4> Endocytosis</h4>
<ul>
<li>Cell takes in materials</li>
<li>Membrane surrounds substance</li>
</ul>
<h4> Exocytosis</h4>
<ul>
<li>Cell releases materials</li>
<li>Vesicles fuse with membrane</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> REAL EXAMPLES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Cancer:</b> uncontrolled cell division forms tumors in organs.</li>
<li><b>Stem cells:</b> used to repair damaged skin or blood cells.</li>
<li><b>Endocytosis:</b> white blood cells engulf bacteria.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> SUMMARY</h3>
<pre>
Mitosis → Growth & repair
Cancer → uncontrolled division
Stem cells → repair system
Transport → cell intake & output
</pre>
', 1),
  (174, 174, '<h2>Biological Molecules</h2>
<p>Biological molecules are chemicals found in living organisms that are essential for life processes such as growth, energy, and repair.</p>
<h3> MAIN BIOLOGICAL MOLECULES</h3>
<pre>
Carbohydrates → Energy
Proteins → Growth & repair
Lipids → Energy storage
Water → Life medium
Vitamins → Protection & regulation
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> 1. CARBOHYDRATES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Main source of energy</li>
<li>Made of carbon, hydrogen, oxygen</li>
<li>Broken down into glucose</li>
</ul>
<h4>Examples:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Starch (potatoes, maize)</li>
<li>Sugar (fruits, honey)</li>
<li>Glycogen (animal storage form)</li>
</ul>
<h3> 2. PROTEINS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Used for growth and repair</li>
<li>Made of amino acids</li>
<li>Contain nitrogen</li>
</ul>
<h4>Examples:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Meat</li>
<li>Eggs</li>
<li>Beans</li>
</ul>
<h3> 3. LIPIDS (FATS & OILS)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Long-term energy storage</li>
<li>Insulation and protection</li>
</ul>
<h4>Examples:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Vegetable oil</li>
<li>Butter</li>
<li>Animal fat</li>
</ul>
<h3> 4. WATER</h3>
<ul>
<li>Solvent for reactions</li>
<li>Transport medium</li>
<li>Temperature regulation</li>
</ul>
<h3> 5. VITAMINS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Needed in small amounts</li>
<li>Prevent diseases</li>
</ul>
<h3> 6. ENZYMES (VERY IMPORTANT)</h3>
<p>Enzymes are biological catalysts that speed up chemical reactions.</p>
<ul>
<li>Work at specific temperatures</li>
<li>Very sensitive to pH</li>
<li>Reusable</li>
</ul>
<h4>Examples:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Amylase → breaks starch</li>
<li>Protease → breaks proteins</li>
<li>Lipase → breaks fats</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 7. DNA & RNA (BASICS)</h3>
<ul>
<li>DNA stores genetic information</li>
<li>RNA helps protein synthesis</li>
</ul>
<h3> 8. NUTRITION IN ORGANISMS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Autotrophic (plants make food)</li>
<li>Heterotrophic (animals eat food)</li>
</ul>
<hr>
', 1),
  (175, 175, '<h2>Respiration — Releasing Energy from Food</h2>

<p>Cells need energy to carry out activities such as movement, growth, repair and active transport.</p>

<p><b>Respiration</b> is the process by which cells release energy from food, especially glucose.</p>

<hr>

<h3>Why is respiration important?</h3>

<ul>
<li>Provides energy for muscle contraction.</li>
<li>Provides energy for active transport.</li>
<li>Provides energy needed for growth and repair.</li>
<li>Provides energy for other activities carried out by cells.</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3>Glucose as an energy source</h3>

<p>Glucose contains stored chemical energy. During respiration, this energy is released and made available to the cell.</p>

<pre>
Glucose → Energy (ATP) + Waste products
</pre>

<hr>

<h3>Types of Respiration</h3>

<ul>
<li><b>Aerobic respiration</b> — uses oxygen, releases more energy</li>
<li><b>Anaerobic respiration</b> — no oxygen, releases less energy</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3>Where does respiration occur?</h3>

<p>Respiration occurs in the <b>mitochondria</b> of every living cell.</p>

<pre>
Mitochondria = "Powerhouse of the Cell"
</pre>
', 1),
  (176, 176, '<h2>Aerobic Respiration</h2>

<p><b>Aerobic respiration</b> is the process of releasing energy from glucose using oxygen.</p>

<hr>

<h3>Word Equation</h3>

<pre>
Glucose + Oxygen → Carbon dioxide + Water + Energy (ATP)
</pre>

<h3>Chemical Equation</h3>

<pre>
C6H12O6 + 6O2 → 6CO2 + 6H2O + ATP
</pre>

<hr>

<h3>Key Facts</h3>

<ul>
<li>Occurs in the <b>mitochondria</b>.</li>
<li>Requires oxygen.</li>
<li>Produces a <b>large amount of energy (ATP)</b>.</li>
<li>Produces carbon dioxide and water as waste.</li>
<li>Efficient process — preferred by the body.</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3>Where the products go</h3>

<ul>
<li><b>Carbon dioxide</b> → exhaled from lungs</li>
<li><b>Water</b> → used in cells or excreted</li>
<li><b>ATP (energy)</b> → used for all cell activities</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3>Conditions needed</h3>

<pre>
1. Glucose (from food)
2. Oxygen (from breathing)
3. Functioning mitochondria
</pre>
', 1),
  (177, 177, '<h2>Anaerobic Respiration &amp; Oxygen Debt</h2>

<p><b>Anaerobic respiration</b> is the release of energy from glucose without using oxygen.</p>

<hr>

<h3>Word Equation (in animals/humans)</h3>

<pre>
Glucose → Lactic acid + Small amount of energy (ATP)
</pre>

<h3>Word Equation (in yeast/plants)</h3>

<pre>
Glucose → Ethanol + Carbon dioxide + Small amount of energy
</pre>

<hr>

<h3>When does anaerobic respiration occur?</h3>

<ul>
<li>During <b>intense exercise</b> when oxygen supply is insufficient.</li>
<li>When muscles need energy faster than aerobic respiration can provide.</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3>Effects of Anaerobic Respiration</h3>

<ul>
<li>Lactic acid builds up in muscles — causes <b>muscle fatigue and cramp</b>.</li>
<li>Less energy (ATP) produced compared to aerobic respiration.</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3>Oxygen Debt</h3>

<p><b>Oxygen debt</b> is the extra oxygen required after exercise to break down the lactic acid that accumulated during anaerobic respiration.</p>

<pre>
Lactic acid + Oxygen → Carbon dioxide + Water
</pre>

<ul>
<li>Causes <b>heavy breathing</b> after exercise.</li>
<li>Heart rate stays elevated to deliver more oxygen.</li>
<li>Lactic acid is transported to the liver for removal.</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3>Comparison: Aerobic vs Anaerobic</h3>

<pre>
Feature          | Aerobic          | Anaerobic
Oxygen needed    | Yes              | No
Energy produced  | Large (many ATP) | Small (few ATP)
Products         | CO2 + Water      | Lactic acid
Location         | Mitochondria     | Cytoplasm
</pre>
', 1),
  (178, 178, '<h2>Excretion — Removing Metabolic Waste</h2>

<p><b>Excretion</b> is the removal of metabolic waste products from the body.</p>

<p>It is different from egestion (removal of undigested food).</p>

<hr>

<h3>Metabolic Waste Products</h3>

<ul>
<li><b>Carbon dioxide (CO2)</b> — from respiration, excreted by lungs</li>
<li><b>Urea</b> — from breakdown of excess amino acids in liver, excreted by kidneys</li>
<li><b>Water</b> — excreted by kidneys, lungs and skin</li>
<li><b>Salts</b> — excreted by kidneys and skin (sweat)</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3>Organs of Excretion</h3>

<pre>
Organ     | Waste Excreted
Lungs     | CO2 and water vapour
Kidneys   | Urea, excess water, salts
Skin      | Small amounts of urea, salts (sweat)
Liver     | Converts excess amino acids into urea (deamination)
</pre>

<hr>

<h3>Why excretion is important</h3>

<ul>
<li>Removes toxic waste that could harm cells.</li>
<li>Maintains correct concentration of substances in the blood.</li>
<li>Helps regulate body temperature (sweating).</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3>Excretion vs Egestion</h3>

<pre>
Excretion = removal of METABOLIC waste (produced by body processes)
Egestion  = removal of UNDIGESTED food through faeces
</pre>
', 1),
  (179, 179, '<h2>The Kidney</h2>

<p>The kidneys are the main organs of excretion. Humans have two kidneys located on either side of the spine.</p>

<hr>

<h3>Functions of the Kidney</h3>

<ul>
<li>Filter blood to remove urea, excess water and salts.</li>
<li>Regulate water content of the blood (osmoregulation).</li>
<li>Maintain salt balance in the body.</li>
<li>Produce urine.</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3>Structure of the Kidney</h3>

<pre>
Outer layer → Cortex (contains glomeruli and Bowman''s capsules)
Inner layer → Medulla (contains tubules and collecting ducts)
Centre      → Pelvis (collects urine)
Ureter → Bladder → Urethra → Outside body
</pre>

<hr>

<h3>Blood Supply to the Kidney</h3>

<ul>
<li><b>Renal artery</b> — carries blood with waste into the kidney.</li>
<li><b>Renal vein</b> — carries cleaned blood away from the kidney.</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3>Kidney Diseases</h3>

<ul>
<li><b>Kidney failure</b> — kidneys cannot filter blood properly.</li>
<li>Treated by <b>dialysis</b> or <b>kidney transplant</b>.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (180, 180, '<h2>The Nephron — Filtration and Reabsorption</h2>

<p>The <b>nephron</b> is the functional unit of the kidney. Each kidney contains about one million nephrons.</p>

<hr>

<h3>Structure of the Nephron</h3>

<pre>
Glomerulus → Bowman''s capsule → Proximal convoluted tubule
→ Loop of Henle → Distal convoluted tubule → Collecting duct → Pelvis
</pre>

<hr>

<h3>Three Processes in the Nephron</h3>

<h4>1. Ultrafiltration</h4>
<ul>
<li>Blood is filtered under high pressure in the <b>glomerulus</b>.</li>
<li>Small molecules pass into the Bowman''s capsule: water, urea, glucose, salts.</li>
<li>Large molecules stay in blood: proteins, red blood cells.</li>
</ul>

<h4>2. Selective Reabsorption</h4>
<ul>
<li>Useful substances are reabsorbed back into the blood.</li>
<li>All glucose is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule.</li>
<li>Water is reabsorbed in the tubules and collecting duct.</li>
<li>Some salts are reabsorbed.</li>
</ul>

<h4>3. Secretion</h4>
<ul>
<li>Additional waste (e.g. excess hydrogen ions) is secreted into the tubule.</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3>What becomes urine?</h3>

<pre>
Remaining fluid → concentrated urea, excess water, salts → Urine
</pre>

<ul>
<li>Urine drains into the <b>collecting duct → pelvis → ureter → bladder</b>.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (181, 181, '<h2>Urine Formation &amp; Dialysis</h2>

<hr>

<h3>Composition of Urine</h3>

<ul>
<li>Water (about 95%)</li>
<li>Urea (waste from protein breakdown)</li>
<li>Excess salts</li>
<li>Small amounts of other waste</li>
</ul>

<p>Normal urine does <b>NOT</b> contain glucose or proteins.</p>

<hr>

<h3>How Urine Formation Works</h3>

<pre>
Blood enters kidney via renal artery
Filtered in glomerulus (ultrafiltration)
Useful substances reabsorbed in tubules
Remaining fluid = urine
Urine → pelvis → ureter → bladder → excreted
</pre>

<hr>

<h3>Dialysis — Treatment for Kidney Failure</h3>

<p><b>Dialysis</b> is the artificial filtration of blood when kidneys fail to function.</p>

<h4>How dialysis works:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Blood is taken out of the body.</li>
<li>Passed through a machine containing a <b>dialysis membrane</b>.</li>
<li>Waste (urea, excess salts) diffuses out through the membrane.</li>
<li>Clean blood is returned to the body.</li>
</ul>

<pre>
Blood with waste → Dialysis machine → Cleaned blood returned
</pre>

<hr>

<h3>Kidney Transplant</h3>
<ul>
<li>A healthy donor kidney replaces the failed kidney.</li>
<li>Permanent solution but requires matching donor.</li>
<li>Patient must take immunosuppressant drugs to prevent rejection.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (182, 182, '<h2>Immune System — Phagocytosis</h2>

<p>The <b>immune system</b> protects the body from disease-causing organisms called <b>pathogens</b>.</p>

<hr>

<h3>Lines of Defence</h3>

<pre>
1st Line → Skin, mucus, cilia (physical barriers — prevent entry)
2nd Line → Phagocytosis (non-specific — destroys invaders)
3rd Line → Antibodies (specific — targets exact pathogens)
</pre>

<hr>

<h3>Phagocytosis</h3>

<p><b>Phagocytosis</b> is the process by which white blood cells (phagocytes) engulf and destroy pathogens.</p>

<h4>Steps of Phagocytosis:</h4>
<ol>
<li>Pathogen enters the body.</li>
<li>Phagocyte detects and moves toward the pathogen.</li>
<li>Phagocyte engulfs the pathogen into a <b>vacuole</b>.</li>
<li>Enzymes (lysosomes) are released to destroy the pathogen.</li>
<li>Digested remains are expelled.</li>
</ol>

<hr>

<h3>Types of White Blood Cells</h3>

<ul>
<li><b>Phagocytes</b> — engulf pathogens (non-specific defence)</li>
<li><b>Lymphocytes</b> — produce antibodies (specific defence)</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3>Why is phagocytosis non-specific?</h3>

<p>Phagocytes engulf any foreign particle — they do not target specific pathogens. This is why it is called non-specific immunity.</p>
', 1),
  (183, 183, '<h2>Antigens, Antibodies &amp; Immunity</h2>

<hr>

<h3>Antigens</h3>

<p>An <b>antigen</b> is a foreign substance (usually a protein) on the surface of a pathogen that triggers an immune response.</p>

<ul>
<li>Each pathogen has unique antigens.</li>
<li>The body recognises them as "non-self" and attacks.</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3>Antibodies</h3>

<p>An <b>antibody</b> is a protein produced by lymphocytes in response to a specific antigen.</p>

<ul>
<li>Each antibody is specific to one antigen.</li>
<li>Antibodies bind to antigens and neutralise the pathogen.</li>
</ul>

<pre>
Antigen (on pathogen) + Antibody → Pathogen destroyed
</pre>

<hr>

<h3>Types of Immunity</h3>

<h4>Active Immunity</h4>
<ul>
<li>Body produces its <b>own antibodies</b>.</li>
<li>Occurs after infection or vaccination.</li>
<li><b>Long-lasting</b> — memory cells are formed.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Passive Immunity</h4>
<ul>
<li>Antibodies are <b>received from another source</b>.</li>
<li>Examples: mother''s antibodies to baby (breast milk/placenta), injection.</li>
<li><b>Short-term</b> — no memory cells formed.</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3>Vaccination</h3>

<p>A vaccine introduces a weakened or dead pathogen into the body, stimulating the immune system to produce antibodies and memory cells without causing disease.</p>

<pre>
Vaccine → Immune response → Memory cells → Future protection
</pre>

<hr>

<h3>Memory Cells</h3>

<p>After the first infection, <b>memory cells</b> are produced. If the same pathogen attacks again, the immune response is much faster and stronger.</p>
', 1),
  (184, 184, '<h2>Skeleton — Support, Protection &amp; Movement</h2>

<p>The <b>skeleton</b> is the internal framework of the body, made of bones and cartilage.</p>

<hr>

<h3>Functions of the Skeleton</h3>

<ul>
<li><b>Support</b> — gives the body its shape and supports tissues.</li>
<li><b>Protection</b> — protects vital organs (skull → brain, ribs → heart/lungs).</li>
<li><b>Movement</b> — provides attachment points for muscles.</li>
<li><b>Blood cell production</b> — red and white blood cells made in bone marrow.</li>
<li><b>Mineral storage</b> — stores calcium and phosphorus.</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3>Types of Bones</h3>

<pre>
Long bones  → Femur (thigh bone) — support and movement
Short bones → Carpals (wrist) — stability
Flat bones  → Skull, ribs — protection
Irregular   → Vertebrae — complex shapes
</pre>

<hr>

<h3>Cartilage</h3>

<ul>
<li>Flexible connective tissue found at joints.</li>
<li>Reduces friction between bones.</li>
<li>Acts as a shock absorber.</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3>Tendons and Ligaments</h3>

<pre>
Tendon   = connects MUSCLE to BONE
Ligament = connects BONE to BONE
</pre>

<ul>
<li><b>Tendons</b> — inelastic, strong cords that transfer muscle force to bones.</li>
<li><b>Ligaments</b> — slightly elastic, hold bones together at joints.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (185, 185, '<h2>Types of Joints</h2>

<p>A <b>joint</b> is a place where two or more bones meet. Joints allow movement.</p>

<hr>

<h3>Classification of Joints</h3>

<h4>1. Fixed (Immovable) Joints</h4>
<ul>
<li>Bones are fused together — no movement.</li>
<li>Example: <b>skull</b> (cranium sutures), <b>pelvis</b>.</li>
</ul>

<h4>2. Slightly Movable Joints</h4>
<ul>
<li>Limited movement.</li>
<li>Example: <b>vertebrae</b> in the spine.</li>
</ul>

<h4>3. Freely Movable (Synovial) Joints</h4>
<ul>
<li>Wide range of movement.</li>
<li>Contain <b>synovial fluid</b> to lubricate the joint.</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3>Types of Synovial Joints</h3>

<pre>
Joint Type      | Movement           | Example
Hinge           | One direction only | Elbow, knee
Ball and socket | All directions     | Shoulder, hip
Pivot           | Rotation only      | Neck (atlas/axis)
Gliding         | Sliding motion     | Wrist carpals
</pre>

<hr>

<h3>Structure of a Synovial Joint</h3>

<ul>
<li><b>Synovial membrane</b> — produces synovial fluid.</li>
<li><b>Synovial fluid</b> — lubricates the joint, reduces friction.</li>
<li><b>Cartilage</b> — covers bone ends, absorbs shock.</li>
<li><b>Ligaments</b> — hold bones together.</li>
<li><b>Capsule</b> — surrounds and protects the joint.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (186, 186, '<h2>Muscles — Antagonistic Muscle Pairs</h2>

<p>Muscles can only <b>pull</b> — they cannot push. This means they must work in pairs to produce movement in opposite directions.</p>

<hr>

<h3>Antagonistic Muscle Pairs</h3>

<p><b>Antagonistic muscles</b> are pairs of muscles that work in opposition — when one contracts, the other relaxes.</p>

<pre>
Biceps contracts (shortens) → Arm bends (flexion)
Triceps relaxes (lengthens)

Triceps contracts (shortens) → Arm straightens (extension)
Biceps relaxes (lengthens)
</pre>

<hr>

<h3>Key Terms</h3>

<ul>
<li><b>Flexor</b> — muscle that bends a joint (e.g. biceps)</li>
<li><b>Extensor</b> — muscle that straightens a joint (e.g. triceps)</li>
<li><b>Contraction</b> — muscle shortens and pulls on bone</li>
<li><b>Relaxation</b> — muscle lengthens and returns to resting length</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3>Examples of Antagonistic Pairs</h3>

<pre>
Body Region | Flexor        | Extensor
Upper arm   | Biceps        | Triceps
Thigh       | Hamstring     | Quadriceps
</pre>

<hr>

<h3>How muscles attach to bones</h3>

<ul>
<li>Muscles are attached to bones by <b>tendons</b>.</li>
<li>The <b>origin</b> is the fixed end of the muscle (does not move).</li>
<li>The <b>insertion</b> is the moving end (attached to the bone that moves).</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (187, 187, '<h2>Body System Integration</h2>

<p>The body''s systems do not work in isolation — they work together (integrate) to maintain life and enable activity.</p>

<hr>

<h3>How Systems Work Together</h3>

<pre>
System       | Role in Integration
Respiratory  | Supplies O2 for aerobic respiration; removes CO2
Circulatory  | Transports O2, CO2, nutrients, hormones, waste
Digestive    | Breaks down food — glucose and nutrients for cells
Excretory    | Removes metabolic waste (urea, CO2, water)
Skeletal     | Framework for movement; blood cell production
Muscular     | Provides movement using energy from respiration
Immune       | Protects all systems from pathogens
Nervous      | Coordinates and controls all systems
Endocrine    | Hormones regulate long-term processes
</pre>

<hr>

<h3>Example: During Exercise</h3>

<pre>
Muscles need more energy (ATP)
More glucose + O2 needed
Heart beats faster → Blood delivers more O2 + glucose to muscles
Lungs breathe faster → More O2 in, more CO2 out
Anaerobic respiration begins if O2 insufficient
Lactic acid builds up → Oxygen debt after exercise
Kidneys excrete excess waste
Skin sweats to cool body temperature
</pre>

<hr>

<h3>Homeostasis</h3>

<p><b>Homeostasis</b> is the maintenance of a constant internal environment despite changes in external conditions.</p>

<ul>
<li>Body temperature maintained at ~37°C</li>
<li>Blood glucose regulated by insulin and glucagon</li>
<li>Water content regulated by kidneys (osmoregulation)</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3>Summary</h3>

<pre>
Respiration → energy (ATP)
Excretion   → waste removal
Immunity    → protection from disease
Muscles     → movement
Skeleton    → support and protection
Nerves      → coordination and control
</pre>
', 1),
  (188, 188, '<h2> Plant Systems </h2>
<p>Focus on the key systems that control plant survival: transport, growth, and distribution.</p>

<hr>

<h3> 1. GROWTH SYSTEM (MERISTEM)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Meristematic tissue → responsible for growth</li>
<li>Located at root and shoot tips</li>
<li>Cells divide rapidly</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 2. XYLEM (WATER TRANSPORT)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Transports water and minerals</li>
<li>Direction: root → leaves (one-way)</li>
<li>Made of dead, hollow cells</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 3. PHLOEM (FOOD TRANSPORT)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Transports sugars (glucose)</li>
<li>Movement: bidirectional</li>
<li>Made of living cells</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 4. TRANSLOCATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Movement of food from source → sink</li>
<li>Source: leaves</li>
<li>Sink: roots, fruits, growing parts</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 5. ACTIVE TRANSPORT</h3>
<ul>
<li>Roots absorb minerals using energy (ATP)</li>
<li>Moves against concentration gradient</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 6. GROWTH STAGES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Cell division</li>
<li>Cell elongation</li>
<li>Cell differentiation</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 7. SEED DISPERSAL</h3>
<ul>
<li>Wind → light seeds</li>
<li>Water → floating seeds</li>
<li>Animals → sticky/edible fruits</li>
<li>Explosion → pods burst</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> CORE FLOW</h3>
<pre>
Roots → absorb water + minerals
Xylem → moves water up
Leaves → make food
Phloem → distributes food
Meristem → growth
</pre>

<hr>

<h3> ULTRA SUMMARY</h3>
<pre>
Xylem → water up
Phloem → food around
Meristem → growth
Translocation → food movement
Active transport → mineral uptake
</pre>
', 1),
  (189, 189, '<h2> Genetics</h2>
<p>Focus on how traits are formed, expressed, and passed on.</p>

<hr>

<h3> 1. CORE UNITS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Gene → unit of inheritance</li>
<li>Alleles → forms of a gene (T / t)</li>
<li>DNA → stores genetic info</li>
<li>Chromosomes → carry genes</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 2. GENOTYPE vs PHENOTYPE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Genotype → genetic code (TT, Tt, tt)</li>
<li>Phenotype → visible trait (tall/short)</li>
</ul>

<pre>
Genotype → hidden
Phenotype → visible
</pre>

<hr>

<h3> 3. DOMINANCE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Dominant (T) → always expressed</li>
<li>Recessive (t) → only if tt</li>
</ul>

<pre>
TT = Tall
Tt = Tall
tt = Short
</pre>

<hr>

<h3>‍‍ 4. INHERITANCE RULE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Each parent gives ONE allele</li>
<li>Offspring = allele combination</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 5. PUNNETT CORE PATTERNS</h3>

<pre>
Tt × Tt → 75% dominant, 25% recessive
TT × tt → 100% dominant
Tt × tt → 50% dominant, 50% recessive
</pre>

<hr>

<h3> 6. VARIATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Continuous → height, weight</li>
<li>Discontinuous → blood group</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 7. MUTATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>DNA change → new traits</li>
<li>Can cause disorders</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 8. NATURAL SELECTION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Best traits survive and reproduce</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> ULTRA SUMMARY</h3>
<pre>
Genes → traits
Alleles → variations
Dominance → what shows
Inheritance → how passed
Punnett → predicts outcomes
Mutation → new traits
Selection → survival advantage
</pre>
', 1),
  (190, 190, '<h1> Genetics & Evolution</h1>
    <p>
      Genetics explains how characteristics are passed from parents to offspring,
      while evolution explains how organisms change gradually over time.
    </p>
    <hr>
      <h2> 1. EVOLUTION</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Evolution</strong> is the gradual change in living organisms over
        many generations. These changes happen because organisms inherit traits
        that help them survive in their environment.
      </p>
        <h3> Key Idea</h3>
        <p>
          Organisms that adapt better to their environment are more likely
          to survive and reproduce.
        </p>
      </div>
      <h3> Darwin’s Theory of Natural Selection</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Overproduction:</strong>
          More offspring are produced than can survive.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Variation:</strong>
          Organisms in the same species have different characteristics.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Competition:</strong>
          Living things compete for food, water, shelter, and mates.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Survival of the Fittest:</strong>
          Organisms with useful adaptations survive longer.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Inheritance:</strong>
          Helpful traits are passed to offspring.
        </li>
      </ol>
        <h3> Examples of Evolution</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Development of antibiotic-resistant bacteria</li>
          <li>Different beak shapes in Darwin’s finches</li>
          <li>Camouflage in insects and animals</li>
          <li>Adaptation of polar bears to cold environments</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
    <hr>
      <h2> 2. NATURAL SELECTION EXAMPLE</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Natural selection</strong> is the process where organisms with
        favorable characteristics survive and reproduce more successfully.
      </p>
        <h3> Giraffe Example</h3>
        <p>
          Long ago, giraffes had different neck lengths. During food shortages,
          giraffes with longer necks could reach leaves high in trees.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Long-necked giraffes survived better</li>
          <li>Short-necked giraffes died more often</li>
          <li>Long-neck traits were passed to offspring</li>
          <li>Over many generations, most giraffes became long-necked</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
        <h3> Important Note</h3>
        <p>
          Natural selection takes place slowly over many generations.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <hr>
      <h2> 3. INHERITANCE</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Inheritance</strong> is the passing of characteristics
        from parents to offspring through genes.
      </p>
        <h3> Key Terms</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Gene:</strong> Unit of heredity found in DNA</li>
          <li><strong>DNA:</strong> Genetic material in cells</li>
          <li><strong>Chromosome:</strong> Structure that carries genes</li>
          <li><strong>Trait:</strong> A characteristic of an organism</li>
          <li><strong>Heredity:</strong> Passing traits from parents to children</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <h3> Examples of Inheritance</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Eye Color:</strong>
          Children may inherit brown, blue, or green eyes from parents.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Blood Group:</strong>
          Blood types such as A, B, AB, and O are inherited genetically.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Height:</strong>
          Height is influenced by both genes and environmental factors such as nutrition.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Hair Texture:</strong>
          Curly or straight hair can be inherited.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Skin Color:</strong>
          Skin color is influenced by multiple genes.
        </li>
      </ul>
        <h3> Remember</h3>
        <p>
          Some characteristics are controlled mainly by genes,
          while others are influenced by both genes and the environment.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
      <h2> Lesson Summary</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Evolution is the gradual change in organisms over time</li>
        <li>Natural selection helps organisms adapt and survive</li>
        <li>Genes carry inherited characteristics</li>
        <li>Traits can be influenced by genes and the environment</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
', 1),
  (191, 191, '<h2> Molecular Genetics</h2>
<p>Focus on how genetic information flows to create traits.</p>
<hr>
<h3> 1. DNA CORE</h3>
<ul>
<li>DNA → genetic material</li>
<li>Bases: A, T, C, G</li>
<li>Pairing: A–T, C–G</li>
<li>Structure: double helix</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 2. DNA REPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>DNA copies itself before cell division</li>
<li>Produces identical DNA molecules</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 3. PROTEIN SYNTHESIS</h3>
<h4>Transcription</h4>
<ul>
<li>DNA → mRNA (in nucleus)</li>
</ul>
<h4>Translation</h4>
<ul>
<li>mRNA → protein (in ribosome)</li>
<li>tRNA brings amino acids</li>
</ul>
<pre>
DNA → RNA → Protein
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> 4. GENETIC CODE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Codon = 3 bases</li>
<li>1 codon → 1 amino acid</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 5. PROTEIN ROLE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Proteins determine traits and functions</li>
</ul>
<pre>
DNA → RNA → Amino acids → Protein → Trait
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> 6. MUTATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Change in DNA sequence</li>
<li>Can alter proteins and traits</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 7. GENETIC ENGINEERING</h3>
<ul>
<li>Humans modify DNA</li>
<li>Used in medicine and agriculture</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 8. CRISPR</h3>
<ul>
<li>Tool for precise gene editing</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 9. SEX-LINKED TRAITS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Genes on X chromosome</li>
<li>Males more affected</li>
<li>Females can be carriers</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> ULTRA SUMMARY</h3>
<pre>
DNA → stores code
Replication → copy DNA
Transcription → DNA to RNA
Translation → RNA to protein
Protein → trait control
Mutation → changes code
Engineering → modify DNA
</pre>
', 1),
  (192, 192, '<h2>Microbiology & Health</h2>
<p>Microbiology is the study of microorganisms and their effects on humans, animals, plants, and the environment.</p>
<hr>
<h3> 1. MICROORGANISMS</h3><li>Such as Bacteria, Viruses, Fungi, Protozoa</li>
<h4> Characteristics:</h4><li>Microscopic (cannot be seen with naked eye) but Can be harmful or beneficial</li>
<hr>
<h3> 2. BACTERIA</h3><li>Single-celled organism that Reproduces quickly</li>
<h4> Useful bacteria:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Decomposition</li>
<li>Food production (yogurt, cheese)</li>
<li>Nitrogen fixation</li>
</ul>
<h4> Harmful bacteria:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Cholera</li>
<li>Tuberculosis</li>
<li>Typhoid</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 3. VIRUSES</h3><li>Not fully living cells but they Need host cell to reproduce</li>
<h4> Diseases:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Flu</li>
<li>HIV/AIDS</li>
<li>COVID-19</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 4. FUNGI</h3><li>Yeast, molds, mushrooms</li>
<h4> Uses:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Bread making</li>
<li>Medicine (antibiotics)</li>
</ul>
<h4> Harmful effects:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Food spoilage</li>
<li>Plant diseases</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 5. PROTOZOA</h3><li>Single-celled organisms</li>
<h4> Diseases:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Malaria</li>
<li>Amoebic dysentery</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 6. DISEASE TRANSMISSION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Air (coughing, sneezing)</li>
<li>Water (contaminated sources)</li>
<li>Food</li>
<li>Vectors (mosquitoes)</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 7. IMMUNITY</h3>
<ul>
<li>Body’s defense against disease</li>
</ul>
<h4>Types:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Natural immunity</li>
<li>Acquired immunity</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 8. VACCINATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Introduces weak pathogen</li>
<li>Helps body build immunity</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 9. ANTIBIOTICS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Drugs that kill bacteria</li>
<li>Do NOT work on viruses</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 10. ANTIBIOTIC RESISTANCE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Bacteria evolve resistance</li>
<li>Caused by misuse of antibiotics</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 11. BIOTECHNOLOGY</h3>
<ul>
<li>Use of organisms to produce useful products</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (193, 193, '<h2> Microbiology & Health Control</h2>
<p>Focus on how diseases are prevented, spread, and controlled.</p>
<hr>
<h3> 1. PREVENTION LEVELS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Primary → prevent disease</li>
<li>Secondary → early detection</li>
<li>Tertiary → reduce damage</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 2. DISEASE SPREAD (EPIDEMIOLOGY)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Pathogen → cause</li>
<li>Host → infected organism</li>
<li>Environment → conditions for spread</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 3. TRANSMISSION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Airborne</li>
<li>Water/foodborne</li>
<li>Vector (mosquitoes)</li>
<li>Direct contact</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 4. FOOD PRESERVATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Cold → slows microbes</li>
<li>Heat → kills microbes</li>
<li>Chemicals → salt/sugar</li>
<li>Modern → pasteurization, canning</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 5. ANTIBIOTICS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Kill or stop bacteria</li>
<li>Do NOT work on viruses</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 6. RESISTANCE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Bacteria adapt and survive drugs</li>
<li>Caused by misuse/overuse</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 7. IMMUNITY</h3>
<ul>
<li>Vaccines → antibodies + memory cells</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Vaccine → Immunity → Protection
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> 8. PUBLIC HEALTH</h3>
<ul>
<li>Sanitation</li>
<li>Health education</li>
<li>Waste management</li>
<li>Vector control</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> ULTRA SUMMARY</h3>
<pre>
Prevent → Primary/Secondary/Tertiary
Spread → Pathogen + Host + Environment
Treat → Antibiotics (bacteria only)
Protect → Vaccines + immunity
Control → Public health systems
</pre>
', 1),
  (194, 194, '<h2>Homeostasis & Reproduction</h2>
<p>This section explains how the body maintains a stable internal environment (homeostasis) and how organisms reproduce to ensure survival of the species.</p>
<hr>
<h3> 1. HOMEOSTASIS (VERY IMPORTANT)</h3>

<h4> Definition</h4>
<p>Homeostasis is the maintenance of a constant internal environment in the body.</p>
<h3> 2. TEMPERATURE REGULATION</h3>
<h4> When body is too hot</h4>
<ul>
<li>Sweating → heat loss</li>
<li>Vasodilation → blood vessels widen</li>
</ul>
<h4> When body is too cold</h4>
<ul>
<li>Shivering → heat production</li>
<li>Vasoconstriction → blood vessels narrow</li>
</ul>
<h3> 3. BLOOD GLUCOSE CONTROL</h3>
<h4> Hormones involved</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Insulin:</b> lowers blood sugar</li>
<li><b>Glucagon:</b> raises blood sugar</li>
</ul>
<h4> Process</h4>
<ul>
<li>High glucose → insulin released → glucose stored as glycogen</li>
<li>Low glucose → glucagon released → glycogen → glucose</li>
</ul>
<pre>
High sugar → Insulin → Storage
Low sugar → Glucagon → Release
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> 4. WATER BALANCE (OSMOREGULATION)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Controlled by kidneys</li>
<li>Hormone: ADH (Antidiuretic hormone)</li>
</ul>
<h4> When dehydrated</h4>
<ul>
<li>More ADH released</li>
<li>More water reabsorbed</li>
<li>Urine becomes concentrated</li>
</ul>
<h4> When hydrated</h4>
<ul>
<li>Less ADH</li>
<li>Less reabsorption</li>
<li>Dilute urine</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 5. NEGATIVE FEEDBACK SYSTEM</h3>
<p>Mechanism that reverses changes to maintain stability.</p>
<pre>
Increase → Response reduces it
Decrease → Response increases it
</pre>
<p><b>Example:</b> Body temperature control</p>
<hr>
<h3> 6. COORDINATION (NERVOUS vs HORMONAL)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Nervous system:</b> fast, short-lived responses</li>
<li><b>Endocrine system:</b> slow, long-lasting responses</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (195, 195, '<h2> Practical Biology</h2>
<p>Focus on enzymes, food tests, and experimental skills.</p>
<hr>
<h3> 1. ENZYMES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Biological catalysts</li>
<li>Speed up reactions without being used</li>
</ul>
<h4>Models</h4>
<ul>
<li>Lock & key → exact fit</li>
<li>Induced fit → flexible fit</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 2. ENZYME FACTORS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Temperature → rise then denature</li>
<li>pH → optimum range</li>
<li>Substrate → increase then plateau</li>
<li>Enzyme concentration → faster rate</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 3. GRAPH PATTERNS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Temperature → increase then drop</li>
<li>Substrate → increase then plateau</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 4. FOOD TESTS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Iodine → starch (blue-black)</li>
<li>Benedict’s + heat → sugar (color change)</li>
<li>Biuret → protein (purple)</li>
<li>Ethanol → lipids (milky)</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 5. KEY EXPERIMENTS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Photosynthesis → iodine test for starch</li>
<li>Transpiration → cobalt chloride (blue → pink)</li>
<li>Enzyme → starch breakdown over time</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 6. MICROSCOPE</h3>
<pre>
Magnification = Image size / Actual size
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> 7. EXPERIMENT DESIGN</h3>
<ul>
<li>Independent → change</li>
<li>Dependent → measure</li>
<li>Control → constant</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Hypothesis → Method → Results → Conclusion
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> 8. DATA INTERPRETATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Identify trends</li>
<li>Explain patterns</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> ULTRA SUMMARY</h3>
<pre>
Enzymes → control reactions
Factors → affect rate
Food tests → identify nutrients
Experiments → prove concepts
Graphs → show patterns
</pre>
', 1),
  (196, 196, '<h2>Practical Biology (Enzymes, ATP & Metabolism)</h2>
<p>This section explains how enzymes are controlled, how energy is transferred in cells, and how metabolic pathways are regulated.</p>
<hr>
<h3> 1. ENZYME INHIBITION (VERY IMPORTANT)</h3>
<p>Enzyme inhibitors reduce or stop enzyme activity.</p>
<hr>
<h3> COMPETITIVE INHIBITION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Inhibitor has similar shape to substrate</li>
<li>Competes for active site</li>
<li>Effect can be reduced by increasing substrate</li>
</ul>
<h3> NON-COMPETITIVE INHIBITION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Inhibitor binds away from active site</li>
<li>Changes enzyme shape</li>
<li>Cannot be reversed by more substrate</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> KEY DIFFERENCE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Competitive → blocks active site</li>
<li>Non-competitive → changes enzyme shape</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 2. COFACTORS & COENZYMES</h3>
<h4> Cofactors</h4>
<ul>
<li>Inorganic ions (e.g. Mg²⁺, Zn²⁺)</li>
<li>Help enzymes function</li>
</ul>
<h4> Coenzymes</h4>
<ul>
<li>Organic molecules (often vitamins)</li>
<li>Assist enzyme reactions</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 3. ATP (ENERGY CURRENCY OF THE CELL)</h3>
<p>ATP stores and transfers energy in cells.</p>
<h4> Process:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Energy released when ATP breaks down</li>
<li>Energy used for cell activities</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 4. ENERGY TRANSFER IN CELLS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Respiration produces ATP</li>
<li>ATP powers:
  <ul>
    <li>Muscle contraction</li>
    <li>Active transport</li>
    <li>Protein synthesis</li>
  </ul>
</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 5. METABOLISM</h3>
<p>All chemical reactions in cells.</p>
<h4>Types:</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Anabolism:</b> building molecules (requires energy)</li>
<li><b>Catabolism:</b> breaking molecules (releases energy)</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 6. ENZYME REGULATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Controlled by temperature</li>
<li>Controlled by pH</li>
<li>Controlled by inhibitors</li>
</ul>
<h3> 7. LINK TO RESPIRATION</h3>
<p>Glucose is broken down to produce ATP:</p>
<pre>
Glucose + Oxygen → CO₂ + Water + ATP (Energy)
</pre>
<hr>
', 1),
  (197, 197, '<h2>Biology Exam Skills</h2>
<p>This phase focuses on how biology is tested in exams using graphs, experiments, tables, and unseen scenarios.</p>
<hr>
<h3> 1. GRAPH INTERPRETATION (VERY HIGH MARK AREA)</h3>
<h4> COMMON GRAPH TYPES:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Line graphs (enzyme activity, growth)</li>
<li>Bar graphs (population comparisons)</li>
<li>Curve graphs (photosynthesis, respiration)</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 2. ENZYME ACTIVITY GRAPHS</h3>
<p>Enzymes are affected by temperature and pH.</p>
<h4> KEY POINTS:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Activity increases with temperature up to optimum</li>
<li>After optimum, enzymes denature</li>
<li>pH also affects shape of enzyme</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 3. PHOTOSYNTHESIS GRAPHS</h3>
<pre>
:contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}
</pre>
<h4> FACTORS:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Light intensity</li>
<li>Carbon dioxide concentration</li>
<li>Temperature</li>
</ul>
<p>Limiting factor determines the rate at any point.</p>
<hr>
<h3> 4. POPULATION GROWTH CURVES</h3>
<h4> TYPES:</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>J-shaped curve:</b> rapid exponential growth</li>
<li><b>S-shaped curve:</b> growth slows due to limiting factors</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 5. EXPERIMENT DESIGN (VERY IMPORTANT)</h3>
<h4> STRUCTURE:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Hypothesis (prediction)</li>
<li>Independent variable (changed factor)</li>
<li>Dependent variable (measured factor)</li>
<li>Controlled variables (kept constant)</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 6. SOURCES OF ERROR</h3>
<ul>
<li>Human error (reading mistakes)</li>
<li>Instrument error (faulty equipment)</li>
<li>Environmental changes</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 7. ACCURACY VS PRECISION</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Accuracy:</b> closeness to true value</li>
<li><b>Precision:</b> closeness of repeated results</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 8. RELIABILITY OF EXPERIMENTS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Repeat experiments</li>
<li>Use large sample size</li>
<li>Average results</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 9. DATA INTERPRETATION (UNSEEN QUESTIONS)</h3>
<p>Students are given tables or unfamiliar setups and asked to infer conclusions.</p>
<h4> EXAM STRATEGY:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Look for trends (increase/decrease)</li>
<li>Compare values</li>
<li>Identify patterns</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 10. TYPICAL EXAM TRICKS</h3>
<ul>
<li>“Explain results” → link to biology theory</li>
<li>“Suggest improvement” → mention reliability or control variables</li>
<li>“Predict outcome” → use patterns</li>
</ul>
<hr>
', 1),
  (198, 198, '<h2>The Nervous System (Structure, Function & Signal Transmission)</h2>
<p>The nervous system is the body''s <b>fast control and communication system</b>. It uses electrical signals called <b>nerve impulses</b> to send information instantly across the body.</p>

<hr>

<h3> 1. MAIN PARTS OF THE NERVOUS SYSTEM</h3>

<h4> A. Brain</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Control center of the body</li>
  <li>Interprets information from senses</li>
  <li>Makes decisions and sends instructions</li>
</ul>

<h4> B. Spinal Cord</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Connects brain to the rest of the body</li>
  <li>Controls reflex actions</li>
  <li>Protected by vertebral column (backbone)</li>
</ul>

<h4> C. Nerves</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Carry messages between body and brain</li>
  <li>Made up of neurons</li>
  <li>Spread throughout the body</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 2. STRUCTURE OF A NEURON (NERVE CELL)</h3>

<div style="text-align:center; margin: 14px 0;">
  <img src="/images/neuron.png" alt="Structure of a Neuron" style="width:100%; max-width:340px; max-height:220px; object-fit:contain; border-radius:10px; box-shadow: 0 3px 12px rgba(0,0,0,0.45); display:block; margin:0 auto;" />
  <p style="font-size:0.8em; color:#a0a0b0; margin-top:6px;"><b>Fig:</b> Neuron (Nerve Cell) Structure</p>
</div>

<ul>
  <li><b>Dendrites:</b> Receive signals from other cells</li>
  <li><b>Cell body:</b> Processes information</li>
  <li><b>Axon:</b> Sends impulses away from the cell body</li>
  <li><b>Myelin sheath:</b> Insulates axon and speeds up signals</li>
  <li><b>Axon terminals:</b> Pass signal to next neuron or muscle</li>
</ul>

<pre>
Dendrites → Cell body → Axon → Axon terminals
</pre>

<hr>

<h3> 3. HOW NERVE IMPULSES TRAVEL</h3>

<p>Nerve impulses are electrical signals that move through neurons.</p>

<h4> Step-by-step process:</h4>

<ul>
  <li>Stimulus is detected by receptors (e.g., skin)</li>
  <li>Signal enters sensory neuron</li>
  <li>Signal goes to brain or spinal cord</li>
  <li>Brain processes the information</li>
  <li>Motor neuron sends response</li>
  <li>Effector (muscle/gland) reacts</li>
</ul>

<pre>
Stimulus → Receptor → Sensory neuron → CNS → Motor neuron → Effector → Response
</pre>

<hr>

<h3> 4. TYPES OF NERVES</h3>

<h4> A. Sensory Nerves</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Carry signals from receptors to brain/spinal cord</li>
  <li>Example: touching hot object</li>
</ul>

<h4> B. Motor Nerves</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Carry signals from brain/spinal cord to muscles</li>
  <li>Example: pulling hand away</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 5. FAST RESPONSE SYSTEM</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Nervous system works in milliseconds</li>
  <li>Uses electrical impulses (very fast)</li>
  <li>Helps protect the body from danger</li>
</ul>

<pre>
Example:
Touch fire → instant withdrawal of hand
</pre>

<hr>

<h3> 6. CENTRAL NERVOUS SYSTEM (CNS)</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Made up of brain and spinal cord</li>
  <li>Processes all information</li>
  <li>Controls voluntary and involuntary actions</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 7. PERIPHERAL NERVOUS SYSTEM (PNS)</h3>

<ul>
  <li>All nerves outside brain and spinal cord</li>
  <li>Connects CNS to organs and muscles</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 8. EXAMPLE OF NERVOUS RESPONSE</h3>

<p><b>Touching a hot object:</b></p>

<ul>
  <li>Skin receptors detect heat</li>
  <li>Sensory neuron sends signal</li>
  <li>Spinal cord processes reflex</li>
  <li>Motor neuron sends message</li>
  <li>Muscles contract and hand moves away</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> REAL-LIFE IMPORTANCE</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Helps avoid danger quickly</li>
  <li>Controls movement and coordination</li>
  <li>Enables thinking and memory</li>
  <li>Controls body functions like breathing and heartbeat</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> SUMMARY</h3>

<pre>
Nervous system = fast communication system
Brain = control center
Spinal cord = reflex control
Nerves = message carriers
Neurons = basic unit of the nervous system
</pre>
